WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:12.000
Hello, everyone, and welcome
to lecture 2 of CME 296.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.680
So today is a very
exciting day because we'll

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:18.000
be talking about a new
generation paradigm, which

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.160
is going to be score matching.

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:26.360
So if you remember last week, we
saw a first generation paradigm

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:30.520
which was diffusion with DDPM.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:34.480
So today we're going to
see a new way of doing this

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:37.680
and see how this relates
to the way we were doing

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:41.520
this with diffusion with DDPM.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.840
So before we start,
I'm just going

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:47.800
to recap what we saw last week.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:52.240
So if you remember, the
first three lectures

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:58.720
are all about understanding
how we can generate new samples

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:03.350
by trying to generate
from a distribution pdata

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:07.670
that we do not know that
is potentially complicated.

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:10.210
So here we have
nothing as input.

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:13.710
So it's unconditioned
generation.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:19.950
And our goal is to learn a
way to generate new images.

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:22.830
So what did we see last time?

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:27.630
So our strategy
was to take images

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:30.910
from the training set that
we called clean because they

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:33.310
did not have any kind of noise.

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:38.550
And what we did is we
defined a process q that

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:44.570
added some noise progressively,
such that at the end of the day,

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:49.550
we ended up with just pure
noise or almost pure noise.

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:55.870
And the goal of DDPM was to
learn a reverse process that

00:01:55.870 --> 00:02:01.100
was able to predict the noise
to remove from the noisy image.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:03.140
So that was the strategy.

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:04.580
You take an image.

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:06.580
You gradually add some noise.

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:12.300
And what you do is you try to
learn how given a noisy image

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:15.900
and given the noise
level, how you can remove

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:19.540
the noise from that image.

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:22.940
So the way we went
about doing this

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:26.660
was through
likelihood estimation.

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:31.020
So we had our model p theta
that we're trying to learn.

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:34.700
And what we were doing
is trying to maximize

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:38.100
the probability of our model
seeing the data from a training

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:39.340
set.

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:42.040
But we saw that it
was intractable.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:47.180
So what we did is we derived
a tractable lower bound

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:49.340
that we could also
maximize that could be

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:52.900
a good proxy for our objective.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:58.640
And we ended up with
this ELBO formulation.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:05.600
And what we did is we expanded
the terms from the lower bound.

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:09.360
And what we saw was that
there was some nice terms that

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:10.540
came out of it.

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:12.840
So there was, for
instance, a KL divergence

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:15.840
between exactly what
we wanted to learn

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:20.440
and the forward process that
we defined that was something

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:24.120
that we could express.

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:28.200
And then the third
step was to indeed show

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:30.400
that this was tractable.

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:35.080
And we leveraged the fact
that we were actually

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:38.160
defining our forward
process with Gaussian noise

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:40.200
and with Gaussian distributions.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.360
And the good thing with
Gaussian distributions

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:45.920
is there's a lot of nice
properties, which is things

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:47.640
that we leverage here.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:51.840
And at the end of the day,
we deduced a loss function

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:54.670
that was actually very simple.

00:03:54.670 --> 00:03:59.590
It was a simple L2
regression loss on the noise

00:03:59.590 --> 00:04:03.510
that we wanted to remove.

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:05.710
All good so far?

00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:07.990
So this was last time.

00:04:07.990 --> 00:04:10.210
And so this was the
loss that we had.

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:15.150
So we had the the noised image
as an input along with the noise

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:16.269
level.

00:04:16.269 --> 00:04:20.550
And we had our model that we
wanted to train which is here,

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:23.470
the noise predictor--
epsilon theta.

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:27.110
And what we wanted
to do is to have

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:33.110
that match as close as possible
the actual noise that we use.

00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:38.350
And as I mentioned, this way of
doing things was just one way.

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:40.070
And what we're
going to see today

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:47.230
is another way of doing this,
which is called score matching.

00:04:47.230 --> 00:04:50.670
So I'm going to start
with just taking

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:55.180
another perspective of the
problem that we have at stake.

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:58.100
So remember that we
have some training

00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:02.100
sets that contains some images.

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:05.400
So these are for instance, the
Teddy bear images that we have.

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:12.060
And our goal is to sample a new
image from that distribution.

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:18.860
So this set of images, they
come from a complex data

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:23.540
distribution, let's say,
pdata that we do not.

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:30.020
And our goal is
to sample from it.

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:35.180
Now, a very common strategy
that we will again reuse here

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:40.540
is to sample from
an easy distribution

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:43.900
and then to recover
the image that we

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:47.260
want to sample towards the end.

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:54.330
So here this cross this
represents maybe an observation

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:56.750
that we can sample that
is not part of pdata

00:05:56.750 --> 00:05:59.710
but part of a distribution
that is easy to sample from.

00:05:59.710 --> 00:06:02.650
So let's say Gaussian noise.

00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:06.730
So given that what
we're wondering is,

00:06:06.730 --> 00:06:10.530
is there a way to
go from something

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:14.050
that is easy to sample
towards something that

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:15.470
is hard to sample?

00:06:18.050 --> 00:06:20.010
Does that make sense?

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:22.210
It's going to be
a bit more clear.

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:31.370
So let's imagine you have p
of x, which is the probability

00:06:31.370 --> 00:06:36.690
density function that takes in
x, the representation of images

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:40.970
as input, and that tells
you the probability density

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:44.290
of it happening at x.

00:06:44.290 --> 00:06:47.870
So this is what the
figure is showing.

00:06:47.870 --> 00:06:54.710
So here darker colors mean
higher values of p of x.

00:06:54.710 --> 00:07:01.470
So if you were over
here, what would

00:07:01.470 --> 00:07:07.070
be a way for you to
come closer to regions

00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:09.370
higher values of p of x?

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:18.870
So the answer is, let's
take the gradient.

00:07:18.870 --> 00:07:23.870
So just as a reminder,
the gradient of a scalar

00:07:23.870 --> 00:07:26.390
function here that
takes in as inputs

00:07:26.390 --> 00:07:31.750
x, which is multidimensional,
and returns a scalar,

00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:38.910
so the gradient of the function
is the vector of components,

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:41.390
partial derivative of
that function with respect

00:07:41.390 --> 00:07:47.540
to each dimension of the
multidimensional space.

00:07:47.540 --> 00:07:50.300
And what the gradient
here represents

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:58.020
is the direction where this
p of x is higher value.

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:02.500
So here the idea is
if we can have access

00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:06.900
to gradient of p of x,
then from this starting

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:12.600
point, we can maybe go towards
regions higher density.

00:08:17.990 --> 00:08:21.860
But there is a problem.

00:08:21.860 --> 00:08:28.460
The problem is that estimating
the gradient of the probability

00:08:28.460 --> 00:08:32.059
density function is actually
a very hard thing to do.

00:08:32.059 --> 00:08:34.620
And there are a few
reasons for that.

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:38.740
So the first one is that
probability density functions,

00:08:38.740 --> 00:08:42.539
by definition, they
are normalized,

00:08:42.539 --> 00:08:44.500
meaning that if you
take the integral

00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:49.010
towards the whole space,
the integral of p of x

00:08:49.010 --> 00:08:51.650
needs to be equal to 1.

00:08:51.650 --> 00:08:55.410
So you have this
constant, that maybe we

00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:57.970
can note here z,
that is something

00:08:57.970 --> 00:09:00.590
that is very hard, intractable
for you to compute,

00:09:00.590 --> 00:09:04.170
because you would need to sum
across all possible values

00:09:04.170 --> 00:09:04.990
of your space.

00:09:04.990 --> 00:09:07.850
And space is
multi-dimensional, and it's

00:09:07.850 --> 00:09:09.370
a lot of computation,
not something

00:09:09.370 --> 00:09:11.930
that you can actually do.

00:09:11.930 --> 00:09:13.790
So this quantity is intractable.

00:09:13.790 --> 00:09:19.530
So if p of x is intractable,
then if you take the gradient,

00:09:19.530 --> 00:09:23.130
it's also intractable.

00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:24.430
So that's the first reason.

00:09:24.430 --> 00:09:30.250
The second reason is different
compared to the first one, which

00:09:30.250 --> 00:09:36.290
is due to the fact that the
probability density function can

00:09:36.290 --> 00:09:39.930
take very low values in
certain regions of space

00:09:39.930 --> 00:09:41.690
where there are very few data.

00:09:41.690 --> 00:09:43.800
So let's suppose here--

00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:46.920
let's say you have a point
here, then the probability

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:49.080
would be very small.

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:56.880
And so this can cause some
numerical stability issues.

00:09:56.880 --> 00:10:00.200
So because of these
reasons, maybe we

00:10:00.200 --> 00:10:05.360
can take a look at another
candidate of a way for you

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:09.480
to go from a certain
point towards regions

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:12.880
of high density of the space.

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:16.680
And that potential candidate
can be the gradient

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:21.760
of the log of the probability,
as opposed to just the gradient

00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:25.240
of the probability.

00:10:25.240 --> 00:10:31.240
So why could that be-- why
would that be a good candidate?

00:10:31.240 --> 00:10:34.680
Well, do you remember
the first point

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:39.000
I mentioned regarding
the normalizing constant?

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:43.780
Well, thanks to the properties
of the logarithm function,

00:10:43.780 --> 00:10:48.740
if you take the logarithm of the
numerator over the denominator,

00:10:48.740 --> 00:10:52.140
it's just the logarithm of the
numerator minus the logarithm

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:53.860
of the denominator.

00:10:53.860 --> 00:10:57.020
And given that the
normalizing constant

00:10:57.020 --> 00:11:03.540
is constant with respect
to x, the point in space,

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:08.380
the gradient of log
of z is actually 0.

00:11:08.380 --> 00:11:14.220
So what that means is in order
to compute the gradient of log

00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:18.020
of p, you don't
have to figure out

00:11:18.020 --> 00:11:23.260
how you want to compute
that intractable quantity.

00:11:23.260 --> 00:11:26.100
So that's reason number 1.

00:11:26.100 --> 00:11:29.120
Reason number 2, I'm not sure
if this was obvious to you,

00:11:29.120 --> 00:11:33.420
but just to emphasize
on this a bit more,

00:11:33.420 --> 00:11:36.500
the gradient of
the log is actually

00:11:36.500 --> 00:11:40.850
pointing in the same direction
as the gradient of p.

00:11:40.850 --> 00:11:44.450
And the reason for that
is just a calculus reason.

00:11:44.450 --> 00:11:47.690
So the gradient of log of
p is equal to the gradient

00:11:47.690 --> 00:11:50.490
of p over p.

00:11:50.490 --> 00:11:58.450
And so what that means is
up to some normalization,

00:11:58.450 --> 00:12:01.210
the gradient of the
log of p is pointing

00:12:01.210 --> 00:12:04.510
towards the same direction
of gradient of p.

00:12:04.510 --> 00:12:05.590
Because p is a scalar.

00:12:05.590 --> 00:12:06.910
Just remember p is a scalar.

00:12:06.910 --> 00:12:13.970
So it's as if you were dividing
your vector by the same quantity

00:12:13.970 --> 00:12:15.910
across all dimensions.

00:12:18.970 --> 00:12:21.890
First reason, it is tractable.

00:12:21.890 --> 00:12:24.850
Second reason, it is pointing
in the same direction

00:12:24.850 --> 00:12:27.050
as gradient of p.

00:12:27.050 --> 00:12:31.130
And then the third reason is
that it is just more numerically

00:12:31.130 --> 00:12:33.210
stable.

00:12:33.210 --> 00:12:38.760
And the reason for that
is when gradient of p

00:12:38.760 --> 00:12:41.880
is very, very small,
then there are chances

00:12:41.880 --> 00:12:44.200
that p is also very small.

00:12:44.200 --> 00:12:49.460
And so if you take the
ratio, it is not as small.

00:12:52.120 --> 00:12:54.920
So that's the third reason.

00:12:54.920 --> 00:13:00.480
So for all of these reasons,
a possible strategy for you

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:04.680
to go from one point
in space towards region

00:13:04.680 --> 00:13:10.960
higher densities with respect to
the probability density function

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:16.640
can be to follow the direction
given by gradient of log

00:13:16.640 --> 00:13:22.160
of p, which we call the score.

00:13:22.160 --> 00:13:24.280
So this is the reason
why this lecture

00:13:24.280 --> 00:13:26.360
is called score matching.

00:13:26.360 --> 00:13:29.200
It is because we're interested
in this quantity, which

00:13:29.200 --> 00:13:32.520
is the score, which is the
gradient of the log of p.

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:37.870
One note that I will add is
just see that I said gradient

00:13:37.870 --> 00:13:40.950
of log of p with respect to x.

00:13:40.950 --> 00:13:43.430
So you may think
it is redundant,

00:13:43.430 --> 00:13:47.310
but it is actually because
we want to distinguish it

00:13:47.310 --> 00:13:50.350
from another score,
which is with respect

00:13:50.350 --> 00:13:54.590
to the parameters of a model
that sometimes people use.

00:13:54.590 --> 00:13:57.270
And so I just want
to clarify here

00:13:57.270 --> 00:13:59.550
that we take the
gradient with respect

00:13:59.550 --> 00:14:03.010
to x, which is the
position in the space.

00:14:06.390 --> 00:14:08.770
Sounds good?

00:14:08.770 --> 00:14:09.270
OK.

00:14:09.270 --> 00:14:09.770
Cool.

00:14:09.770 --> 00:14:13.090
So this is how our score
would look like in space.

00:14:13.090 --> 00:14:16.790
So here if you're further
away, typically the score,

00:14:16.790 --> 00:14:19.150
the magnitude would
be bigger because you

00:14:19.150 --> 00:14:21.250
have the gradient of p over p.

00:14:21.250 --> 00:14:26.670
And p is small, and it's
smaller when you get closer

00:14:26.670 --> 00:14:28.790
to the distribution.

00:14:28.790 --> 00:14:32.310
So now a question that
I have for you is,

00:14:32.310 --> 00:14:36.860
let's suppose we sample
from this space, sample

00:14:36.860 --> 00:14:40.740
from a distribution that is easy
to sample, which is somewhere

00:14:40.740 --> 00:14:42.500
in space.

00:14:42.500 --> 00:14:46.180
And if I were to tell
you, if I were to ask you,

00:14:46.180 --> 00:14:50.780
how would you go from this
point to higher density regions

00:14:50.780 --> 00:14:54.260
of the space using the score?

00:14:54.260 --> 00:14:55.220
How would you do it?

00:14:55.220 --> 00:14:58.280
[AUDIO OUT]

00:14:58.780 --> 00:14:59.560
Follow the score.

00:14:59.560 --> 00:15:01.580
Yeah.

00:15:01.580 --> 00:15:03.503
So you could follow the score.

00:15:03.503 --> 00:15:05.420
So the reason why I'm
asking you this question

00:15:05.420 --> 00:15:08.140
is because there is a catch.

00:15:08.140 --> 00:15:10.700
Because if you
follow the score, it

00:15:10.700 --> 00:15:14.220
means that you're going
towards higher density

00:15:14.220 --> 00:15:16.660
regions of the space.

00:15:16.660 --> 00:15:20.420
But the problem is
you may always go

00:15:20.420 --> 00:15:23.420
where the density is highest.

00:15:23.420 --> 00:15:27.460
And so here when you look
at this illustration,

00:15:27.460 --> 00:15:30.080
there are places where
density is not the highest,

00:15:30.080 --> 00:15:34.800
but it is still a valid
sample from the distribution.

00:15:34.800 --> 00:15:37.600
So actually what
we want is not only

00:15:37.600 --> 00:15:42.180
go towards higher density
places of the region,

00:15:42.180 --> 00:15:49.080
but also to have some
diversity, to not always go

00:15:49.080 --> 00:15:51.440
at the same place.

00:15:51.440 --> 00:15:54.080
So that's the reason why.

00:15:54.080 --> 00:15:57.920
Let's assume we know the score.

00:15:57.920 --> 00:16:02.100
We would use a sampling method
that is very commonly used,

00:16:02.100 --> 00:16:06.400
and I'm going to call out now,
which is called the Langevin

00:16:06.400 --> 00:16:07.840
sampling.

00:16:07.840 --> 00:16:11.680
And what that does
is it not only allows

00:16:11.680 --> 00:16:16.860
you to go towards higher
density regions of the space,

00:16:16.860 --> 00:16:20.600
but also have a
stochastic term that

00:16:20.600 --> 00:16:25.080
allows you to go to explore
a little bit the region.

00:16:25.080 --> 00:16:28.960
So I displayed the formula
here on the slide a little bit

00:16:28.960 --> 00:16:30.320
out of nowhere.

00:16:30.320 --> 00:16:32.950
So we won't have
time to derive it.

00:16:32.950 --> 00:16:35.830
But I just want to
show you that there

00:16:35.830 --> 00:16:40.750
is a way for you to start
from a starting point,

00:16:40.750 --> 00:16:45.230
and then use the score
to end up with a sample

00:16:45.230 --> 00:16:49.390
from the probability
distribution.

00:16:49.390 --> 00:16:53.510
And that method is
called Langevin sampling.

00:16:53.510 --> 00:16:57.110
So if you know
about MCMC methods,

00:16:57.110 --> 00:17:00.310
so Markov Chain Monte
Carlo, so Langevin sampling

00:17:00.310 --> 00:17:02.970
is just one such methods.

00:17:05.670 --> 00:17:06.970
That sounds good?

00:17:06.970 --> 00:17:07.470
Yeah.

00:17:07.470 --> 00:17:10.778
[AUDIO OUT]

00:17:12.190 --> 00:17:12.690
Yep.

00:17:12.690 --> 00:17:16.160
[AUDIO OUT]

00:17:26.069 --> 00:17:29.540
Yeah, so the question is,
are these coefficients

00:17:29.540 --> 00:17:30.580
coming out of nowhere?

00:17:30.580 --> 00:17:32.860
Are they're here for a reason?

00:17:32.860 --> 00:17:33.960
The answer is yes.

00:17:33.960 --> 00:17:36.420
They're here for a reason.

00:17:36.420 --> 00:17:39.320
And they can be derived
using some equations.

00:17:39.320 --> 00:17:41.660
So if you've heard
Fokker-Planck equation

00:17:41.660 --> 00:17:44.260
is something that you
can use to derive this.

00:17:44.260 --> 00:17:47.340
We won't have time to derive
it, but there is a reason.

00:17:47.340 --> 00:17:48.780
There is a reason for this.

00:17:48.780 --> 00:17:50.280
Yeah.

00:17:50.280 --> 00:17:53.780
[AUDIO OUT]

00:17:58.420 --> 00:17:59.800
Yes.

00:17:59.800 --> 00:18:04.380
So the question is, is
there a convergence concern

00:18:04.380 --> 00:18:06.740
because the score
is getting lower

00:18:06.740 --> 00:18:08.680
and you always go
from one to the other?

00:18:08.680 --> 00:18:09.980
Is that the question?

00:18:09.980 --> 00:18:13.940
So we will see that
later on and see how

00:18:13.940 --> 00:18:15.980
that is actually not a concern.

00:18:15.980 --> 00:18:17.080
But yeah, great question.

00:18:17.080 --> 00:18:17.580
Yeah.

00:18:17.580 --> 00:18:21.050
[AUDIO OUT]

00:18:28.970 --> 00:18:32.470
So the question is, how
do you choose alpha?

00:18:32.470 --> 00:18:34.970
And we're going to
also see that later.

00:18:34.970 --> 00:18:36.630
But it is something
that you choose.

00:18:40.690 --> 00:18:42.370
Cool.

00:18:42.370 --> 00:18:44.010
Great questions.

00:18:44.010 --> 00:18:47.930
And with that, I for
now assume that we

00:18:47.930 --> 00:18:49.910
knew how to get the score.

00:18:49.910 --> 00:18:53.290
But the reality is we don't.

00:18:53.290 --> 00:18:57.170
So we're going to spend
this first part just trying

00:18:57.170 --> 00:19:00.930
to see how we can
estimate the score.

00:19:00.930 --> 00:19:04.810
And it is actually
not an easy problem,

00:19:04.810 --> 00:19:07.250
and we're going to see why.

00:19:07.250 --> 00:19:11.410
So first of all, the task
of estimating the score

00:19:11.410 --> 00:19:14.370
is widely known
as score matching,

00:19:14.370 --> 00:19:17.330
because you're trying
to match the score.

00:19:17.330 --> 00:19:21.850
So here the way you want to do
it is similar with the noise L2

00:19:21.850 --> 00:19:22.850
regression.

00:19:22.850 --> 00:19:25.850
You also want to have
some L2 regression,

00:19:25.850 --> 00:19:29.070
but this time on the score.

00:19:29.070 --> 00:19:35.030
But the main problem is you
don't know what pdata is,

00:19:35.030 --> 00:19:40.190
and you also do not know the
score of the data distribution.

00:19:40.190 --> 00:19:44.070
So looking at this
loss function,

00:19:44.070 --> 00:19:50.910
there's actually no way for
you to estimate s theta of x.

00:19:50.910 --> 00:19:53.310
So that's the reason
why there's been

00:19:53.310 --> 00:19:56.670
a few papers across the
years, along the years, that

00:19:56.670 --> 00:20:03.830
have tried to estimate this
score function without having

00:20:03.830 --> 00:20:08.230
the need to know the
true score function.

00:20:08.230 --> 00:20:13.630
So I'm just going to enumerate a
few methods that were out there.

00:20:13.630 --> 00:20:16.330
For instance, this
one is back in 2005,

00:20:16.330 --> 00:20:19.790
there was a method called
Implicit Score Matching that

00:20:19.790 --> 00:20:22.150
derived a loss that
actually did not

00:20:22.150 --> 00:20:25.580
require you to have the
knowledge of the true score

00:20:25.580 --> 00:20:26.860
function.

00:20:26.860 --> 00:20:30.700
And there is another method
called Sliced Score Matching,

00:20:30.700 --> 00:20:35.900
which used the
fact of projecting

00:20:35.900 --> 00:20:40.780
the score on different random
vectors, random projections.

00:20:40.780 --> 00:20:45.560
So I put the papers at
the bottom of the slide.

00:20:45.560 --> 00:20:48.140
We're not going to
go into details just

00:20:48.140 --> 00:20:51.140
because these methods
are not really

00:20:51.140 --> 00:20:55.020
commonly used like nowadays.

00:20:55.020 --> 00:20:57.380
But instead what
we're going to do

00:20:57.380 --> 00:21:01.800
is to focus on the common
method that is being used,

00:21:01.800 --> 00:21:05.860
which is called
denoising score matching.

00:21:05.860 --> 00:21:11.260
And it relies on the
following observation.

00:21:11.260 --> 00:21:13.420
So I told you that
we do not have

00:21:13.420 --> 00:21:17.460
the knowledge of the score
of the true distribution,

00:21:17.460 --> 00:21:22.260
because the true distribution
is a complex distribution.

00:21:22.260 --> 00:21:26.090
But I also told you that
Gaussian distributions

00:21:26.090 --> 00:21:28.730
have very nice properties.

00:21:28.730 --> 00:21:31.650
So one idea that we
can have is let's

00:21:31.650 --> 00:21:34.570
try to see if we can compute
the score of a Gaussian

00:21:34.570 --> 00:21:37.130
distribution.

00:21:37.130 --> 00:21:39.450
So let's assume that we
have a random variable

00:21:39.450 --> 00:21:43.930
x that is drawn from a normal
distribution of mean mu

00:21:43.930 --> 00:21:46.190
and variance sigma squared.

00:21:46.190 --> 00:21:50.890
So here I'm taking an example
in 1D just so that we're--

00:21:50.890 --> 00:21:53.890
so that it's easier.

00:21:53.890 --> 00:21:57.010
So the probability distribution
function or probability density

00:21:57.010 --> 00:22:01.870
function is given
by this formula,

00:22:01.870 --> 00:22:05.790
which is analytically explicit.

00:22:05.790 --> 00:22:08.570
It is something
that is given here.

00:22:08.570 --> 00:22:12.610
Well, it turns out
that if you take

00:22:12.610 --> 00:22:17.010
the gradient of
the log of p, then

00:22:17.010 --> 00:22:24.320
you end up with this very
nice formulation of minus x

00:22:24.320 --> 00:22:26.980
minus mu over sigma squared.

00:22:30.680 --> 00:22:36.920
Should we derive it, or is
it, quote unquote, "obvious"?

00:22:36.920 --> 00:22:38.500
You want to derive it?

00:22:38.500 --> 00:22:39.000
No?

00:22:39.000 --> 00:22:40.360
OK.

00:22:40.360 --> 00:22:46.520
Maybe one thing I will
do is to just tell you

00:22:46.520 --> 00:22:49.840
why this formula makes sense.

00:22:49.840 --> 00:22:57.680
So if you consider a
Gaussian distribution,

00:22:57.680 --> 00:23:01.040
the probability density function
would be something like this.

00:23:01.040 --> 00:23:04.980
You'll have high densities
towards the mean.

00:23:04.980 --> 00:23:08.480
And then you will have lower
values of the densities

00:23:08.480 --> 00:23:10.760
as you go away.

00:23:10.760 --> 00:23:12.680
So here what you're
saying is if you

00:23:12.680 --> 00:23:19.700
take some x in space,
what it is saying

00:23:19.700 --> 00:23:26.540
is the score at that point
x is given by minus x

00:23:26.540 --> 00:23:29.900
minus mu over sigma squared.

00:23:29.900 --> 00:23:35.920
So what that means is, so
minus x minus mu is mu minus x.

00:23:35.920 --> 00:23:36.420
Oops.

00:23:36.420 --> 00:23:38.840
So it's supposed to
be a straight line.

00:23:38.840 --> 00:23:40.658
So let's suppose
it's a straight line.

00:23:40.658 --> 00:23:42.200
It's going to be
somewhere like this.

00:23:46.940 --> 00:23:49.360
Sigma squared is positive.

00:23:49.360 --> 00:23:53.060
So it's going to be
pointing towards the mean.

00:23:53.060 --> 00:23:56.220
And just remember
that the score is

00:23:56.220 --> 00:24:01.380
a quantity that gives us the
direction of higher densities

00:24:01.380 --> 00:24:04.060
in the space.

00:24:04.060 --> 00:24:08.040
So just to tell you that
this formula makes sense.

00:24:12.340 --> 00:24:16.980
So back to our original goal.

00:24:16.980 --> 00:24:22.770
So what we want is to
find a way to compute

00:24:22.770 --> 00:24:25.610
the score of a distribution.

00:24:25.610 --> 00:24:29.490
And we saw that computing the
score of a Gaussian distribution

00:24:29.490 --> 00:24:30.210
is easy.

00:24:32.810 --> 00:24:39.690
So how about we take one
point from our training

00:24:39.690 --> 00:24:43.650
set and we noise it.

00:24:43.650 --> 00:24:49.050
So let's suppose we're given
x from our training set.

00:24:49.050 --> 00:24:52.250
And let's suppose we
add some Gaussian noise,

00:24:52.250 --> 00:24:55.850
and we scale it with sigma.

00:24:55.850 --> 00:25:02.570
Then we have x tilde, which is
equal to x plus sigma epsilon.

00:25:02.570 --> 00:25:07.530
So x tilde follows a
normal distribution

00:25:07.530 --> 00:25:12.130
of mean x and variance
sigma squared I.

00:25:12.130 --> 00:25:23.240
And using exactly these results,
you get that the score of--

00:25:23.240 --> 00:25:27.840
so we're calling that q
sigma of x tilde given x.

00:25:27.840 --> 00:25:30.980
So this quantity is actually
something that is tractable.

00:25:35.986 --> 00:25:42.960
So now the question is,
how can we use that result

00:25:42.960 --> 00:25:46.640
in order to go back to our
initial objective, which

00:25:46.640 --> 00:25:51.060
is to determine the score of the
data distribution of interest?

00:25:54.000 --> 00:25:58.120
So just remember at the
first starting point

00:25:58.120 --> 00:26:04.720
we want to estimate the score
of pdata, which is like this.

00:26:04.720 --> 00:26:10.840
Well, the key idea here is to
take that data distribution

00:26:10.840 --> 00:26:13.800
and to noise it.

00:26:13.800 --> 00:26:18.390
So for that, we're
going to introduce

00:26:18.390 --> 00:26:22.690
q sigma of x tilde,
which is this quantity.

00:26:22.690 --> 00:26:25.330
And I'm going to explain
what that quantity is.

00:26:29.950 --> 00:26:35.830
So what I'm going to do is to
just rewrite the expression

00:26:35.830 --> 00:26:39.550
on the blackboard.

00:26:39.550 --> 00:26:40.650
And maybe it's here.

00:26:40.650 --> 00:26:42.690
[AUDIO OUT]

00:26:42.690 --> 00:26:43.190
OK.

00:26:43.190 --> 00:26:45.030
Thank you.

00:26:45.030 --> 00:26:52.710
So you have q sigma of x tilde.

00:26:52.710 --> 00:26:55.390
Whoops.

00:26:55.390 --> 00:27:03.665
So it is a function
of this perturbation.

00:27:03.665 --> 00:27:05.290
So it's called the
perturbation kernel.

00:27:05.290 --> 00:27:09.890
So you take one data point
from your data distribution.

00:27:09.890 --> 00:27:11.550
And then you noise it.

00:27:11.550 --> 00:27:13.350
So this is the one.

00:27:13.350 --> 00:27:15.290
And what you're
actually doing is

00:27:15.290 --> 00:27:20.010
you're repeating that
procedure for all elements

00:27:20.010 --> 00:27:23.610
of your training set.

00:27:23.610 --> 00:27:32.450
Which is why you're ending
up with this distribution.

00:27:32.450 --> 00:27:35.090
So this is an
expression that takes

00:27:35.090 --> 00:27:39.750
in each point of
your training set.

00:27:39.750 --> 00:27:41.450
And that noises it.

00:27:41.450 --> 00:27:44.450
And you just repeat that process
for all points of your training

00:27:44.450 --> 00:27:46.850
set.

00:27:46.850 --> 00:27:54.770
And this you can interpret it as
a joint probability distribution

00:27:54.770 --> 00:28:02.050
that you can also note q
sigma of x tilde and x,

00:28:02.050 --> 00:28:03.970
which is a valid one.

00:28:03.970 --> 00:28:10.470
Because if you integrate over
x tilde and x, you get one.

00:28:13.280 --> 00:28:15.960
So I just want you to
look at this formula

00:28:15.960 --> 00:28:19.340
and just not be scared
about what that means.

00:28:19.340 --> 00:28:21.280
I think it's quite easy.

00:28:21.280 --> 00:28:25.960
Another way of seeing
this is in practice,

00:28:25.960 --> 00:28:30.680
what you have is only a few data
points from your distribution.

00:28:30.680 --> 00:28:35.000
So it's as if you were saying
you take the finite set of data

00:28:35.000 --> 00:28:38.160
points that you have from your
distribution, and what you do

00:28:38.160 --> 00:28:40.640
is you noise each of it.

00:28:40.640 --> 00:28:45.880
So it's as if you were obtaining
a mixture of Gaussians.

00:28:45.880 --> 00:28:51.160
It's as if you were obtaining--
you have your different points.

00:28:51.160 --> 00:28:59.880
And what you do is you
noise, you noise each point.

00:28:59.880 --> 00:29:03.040
And so we're going
to call q sigma of x

00:29:03.040 --> 00:29:08.160
tilde the noisy distribution.

00:29:08.160 --> 00:29:10.510
Yeah?

00:29:10.510 --> 00:29:11.110
Cool.

00:29:11.110 --> 00:29:18.270
So as you can tell, q sigma
is a function of sigma.

00:29:18.270 --> 00:29:20.950
And sigma tells you
how much noise you're

00:29:20.950 --> 00:29:23.030
adding to the distribution.

00:29:23.030 --> 00:29:27.910
So if you have, let's say, a
small sigma, your distribution

00:29:27.910 --> 00:29:29.290
is just a little bit noisy.

00:29:29.290 --> 00:29:32.950
So it just spreads a little bit.

00:29:32.950 --> 00:29:37.110
But then as you add
more and more noise,

00:29:37.110 --> 00:29:43.750
it just spreads out and
takes much more space.

00:29:43.750 --> 00:29:48.590
So how can you use these
definitions in order

00:29:48.590 --> 00:29:52.550
for us to compute
the true score?

00:29:52.550 --> 00:29:56.270
So we're going to see it now.

00:29:56.270 --> 00:30:01.350
So let's consider the
score matching objective

00:30:01.350 --> 00:30:04.750
not on the original
data distribution

00:30:04.750 --> 00:30:07.820
but on the noisy distribution.

00:30:07.820 --> 00:30:12.300
So if we take q
sigma of x tilde,

00:30:12.300 --> 00:30:17.700
we have the squared
distance between the score

00:30:17.700 --> 00:30:21.340
of x tilde and the--

00:30:21.340 --> 00:30:25.740
sorry, the predicted score
and the true score of x tilde.

00:30:25.740 --> 00:30:31.620
Well, there is a paper that
showed that this is actually

00:30:31.620 --> 00:30:37.460
equivalent to optimizing for
another loss function, which

00:30:37.460 --> 00:30:45.100
is this one, which is s theta
of x tilde minus the score,

00:30:45.100 --> 00:30:50.980
but conditioned on each data
points of your training set.

00:30:50.980 --> 00:30:53.860
And so why is this good news?

00:30:53.860 --> 00:30:57.860
It is because that
conditional score is something

00:30:57.860 --> 00:31:00.140
that we already saw.

00:31:00.140 --> 00:31:08.090
It is tractable, but
we have a problem.

00:31:08.090 --> 00:31:10.650
And the problem we're
going to see later.

00:31:10.650 --> 00:31:12.610
Before we do that,
I'm just trying

00:31:12.610 --> 00:31:15.630
to see if we have
time to derive it.

00:31:15.630 --> 00:31:21.310
So the derivation is in between
being trivial and not trivial.

00:31:21.310 --> 00:31:22.730
It's in the middle.

00:31:22.730 --> 00:31:25.410
I would say the
proof is quite nice.

00:31:25.410 --> 00:31:28.572
It is not required for
you to know for the exam.

00:31:28.572 --> 00:31:30.030
So I think in the
interest of time,

00:31:30.030 --> 00:31:31.590
I'm not going to
do the full proof,

00:31:31.590 --> 00:31:36.130
but I'm going to show you
how you can derive it just

00:31:36.130 --> 00:31:38.730
through the main step.

00:31:38.730 --> 00:31:46.090
So what we're saying is we have
the original score matching

00:31:46.090 --> 00:31:55.890
function, which is the
one at the top, which

00:31:55.890 --> 00:32:00.770
is the squared distance
between the score of the noise

00:32:00.770 --> 00:32:08.750
observation minus the
true score of q sigma.

00:32:14.110 --> 00:32:18.670
What we're saying is optimizing
for these loss function

00:32:18.670 --> 00:32:20.850
is the same as
optimizing for this one.

00:32:23.990 --> 00:32:29.010
So the expectation is over
x over your training set

00:32:29.010 --> 00:32:34.590
and then x tilde,
which is given x.

00:32:34.590 --> 00:32:39.050
And so it's
expectation of, again,

00:32:39.050 --> 00:32:41.690
the score of the
noisy observation,

00:32:41.690 --> 00:32:46.790
but this one minus
the true score

00:32:46.790 --> 00:32:51.690
of the conditional
observation condition on x.

00:32:55.630 --> 00:32:57.830
So what you want to
show this is the same

00:32:57.830 --> 00:33:01.350
as optimizing for this,
which is tractable.

00:33:01.350 --> 00:33:05.020
So the trick here is
to recognize the fact

00:33:05.020 --> 00:33:10.580
that you have a distance of
two quantities, let's say, a

00:33:10.580 --> 00:33:22.500
and b, which is something that
you can expand as follows.

00:33:22.500 --> 00:33:26.140
So the distance
between a and b squared

00:33:26.140 --> 00:33:30.980
is a squared minus 2
dot product of a and b

00:33:30.980 --> 00:33:39.700
plus this square norm of b.

00:33:39.700 --> 00:33:43.020
And what you see is--

00:33:43.020 --> 00:33:44.940
so these two loss
functions, you want

00:33:44.940 --> 00:33:48.300
to optimize them with
respect to the parameters

00:33:48.300 --> 00:33:51.140
of the model, which is theta.

00:33:51.140 --> 00:33:56.420
And so you first realize
that the b term in both loss

00:33:56.420 --> 00:34:01.610
functions, they are not
a function of theta.

00:34:01.610 --> 00:34:04.330
So actually you don't care.

00:34:04.330 --> 00:34:06.310
So this one you ignore.

00:34:08.929 --> 00:34:12.969
And then you look at just
the first term, the a term.

00:34:12.969 --> 00:34:16.810
And you just realize that
these terms are the same.

00:34:16.810 --> 00:34:20.010
So this one you also don't care.

00:34:20.010 --> 00:34:22.489
The only thing that
you care is showing

00:34:22.489 --> 00:34:26.090
that the expectation
across x tilde of the dot

00:34:26.090 --> 00:34:31.610
product between the two is
the same as the expectation

00:34:31.610 --> 00:34:40.409
of over x and x tilde of the dot
product between these prediction

00:34:40.409 --> 00:34:43.770
model and this
conditional score.

00:34:43.770 --> 00:34:47.610
You want to show that
these two are equal.

00:34:47.610 --> 00:34:51.130
And this is what the
proof is showing.

00:34:51.130 --> 00:34:55.510
So if you're interested
in the details,

00:34:55.510 --> 00:34:57.730
I would highly encourage
you to take a look

00:34:57.730 --> 00:35:00.680
at the original paper, which
is the denoising score matching

00:35:00.680 --> 00:35:02.760
paper in the appendix.

00:35:02.760 --> 00:35:06.440
So it's maybe a five
or six-line derivation.

00:35:06.440 --> 00:35:10.000
And what it does is
it just leverages

00:35:10.000 --> 00:35:13.320
the definition of q
sigma and then just does

00:35:13.320 --> 00:35:20.760
some nice term expansion
and term identification.

00:35:20.760 --> 00:35:27.240
So all I want to say is
that this equivalence is not

00:35:27.240 --> 00:35:28.100
pure magic.

00:35:28.100 --> 00:35:30.560
It's just math, math.

00:35:30.560 --> 00:35:33.560
And what is great
about it is that we're

00:35:33.560 --> 00:35:38.440
able to optimize the loss
of the score matching

00:35:38.440 --> 00:35:43.180
for the noisy distribution
using a tractable loss.

00:35:43.180 --> 00:35:46.656
[AUDIO OUT]

00:35:53.120 --> 00:35:56.280
So the question is, the
score of the noisy data

00:35:56.280 --> 00:35:59.400
is equal to the score
of the conditional.

00:35:59.400 --> 00:36:06.580
Well, not exactly, but what your
model s theta of x tilde learns

00:36:06.580 --> 00:36:09.020
ends up being the same.

00:36:09.020 --> 00:36:14.460
And the reason for that is
because here what you care about

00:36:14.460 --> 00:36:17.880
is the direction where
you're optimizing your model.

00:36:17.880 --> 00:36:19.940
So you do gradient
descent typically.

00:36:19.940 --> 00:36:23.140
And what you care
about is the gradient

00:36:23.140 --> 00:36:26.780
of that loss with
respect to theta.

00:36:26.780 --> 00:36:29.940
And so that's why we have
this whole derivation

00:36:29.940 --> 00:36:35.580
to just show that the
loss at the bottom

00:36:35.580 --> 00:36:39.820
is equal to the one at the top,
plus some constant that is not

00:36:39.820 --> 00:36:42.460
a function of theta.

00:36:42.460 --> 00:36:45.180
And for that reason,
if you optimize

00:36:45.180 --> 00:36:47.940
the loss that is
tractable from the bottom,

00:36:47.940 --> 00:36:50.880
it's the same as optimizing
the loss from the top.

00:36:50.880 --> 00:36:52.500
[AUDIO OUT]

00:36:52.500 --> 00:36:53.320
They're not equal.

00:36:53.320 --> 00:36:54.200
Yes, exactly.

00:36:54.200 --> 00:36:55.330
Yeah, yeah.

00:36:55.330 --> 00:36:56.690
Great question.

00:36:56.690 --> 00:36:58.470
Any other questions on this?

00:36:58.470 --> 00:36:58.970
Yeah.

00:36:58.970 --> 00:37:02.470
[AUDIO OUT]

00:37:23.970 --> 00:37:24.925
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:37:24.925 --> 00:37:25.550
Great question.

00:37:25.550 --> 00:37:27.258
So the question is,
what is the intuition

00:37:27.258 --> 00:37:28.930
behind this being the same?

00:37:28.930 --> 00:37:31.010
So I think the
intuition goes back

00:37:31.010 --> 00:37:35.570
to the way you define
q sigma and the way

00:37:35.570 --> 00:37:37.470
taking the expectation works.

00:37:40.370 --> 00:37:49.650
So here what you're saying is
if you take a look at scores

00:37:49.650 --> 00:37:53.310
conditioned on points
from the training sets,

00:37:53.310 --> 00:37:58.800
so if you take a look at
this, and then if you somehow

00:37:58.800 --> 00:38:02.640
see all the different scores
from all the different points

00:38:02.640 --> 00:38:06.720
conditioned on these
points, then on average

00:38:06.720 --> 00:38:08.880
is going to be
the same as if you

00:38:08.880 --> 00:38:12.920
were to actually take the
score from the overall noisy

00:38:12.920 --> 00:38:14.360
distribution.

00:38:14.360 --> 00:38:15.740
That's the rough intuition.

00:38:22.000 --> 00:38:23.600
Does that make sense?

00:38:23.600 --> 00:38:25.020
OK.

00:38:25.020 --> 00:38:25.620
OK, cool.

00:38:25.620 --> 00:38:27.800
I'm looking at the time.

00:38:27.800 --> 00:38:31.160
So let's see.

00:38:31.160 --> 00:38:34.480
Yeah, so again, the
proof is not something

00:38:34.480 --> 00:38:36.860
that we will expect in the exam.

00:38:36.860 --> 00:38:41.760
So feel free to just take a look
at it if you're just curious.

00:38:41.760 --> 00:38:45.800
But it's not required.

00:38:45.800 --> 00:38:55.070
So this is also called L of DSM,
so Denoising Score Matching.

00:38:55.070 --> 00:38:58.590
And again this loss is
tractable because you

00:38:58.590 --> 00:39:04.110
know the score of q sigma
of x tilde conditional

00:39:04.110 --> 00:39:08.710
on x, because this is
a score of a Gaussian.

00:39:08.710 --> 00:39:13.950
So just recapping where we
are, what we wanted to do

00:39:13.950 --> 00:39:19.150
is to estimate the score
of the true distribution.

00:39:19.150 --> 00:39:21.510
So what we said was,
OK, it's actually

00:39:21.510 --> 00:39:23.070
not something we can do.

00:39:23.070 --> 00:39:26.070
Let's fall back on
an easier setup.

00:39:26.070 --> 00:39:29.630
And what we said was that
the easier setup was that

00:39:29.630 --> 00:39:34.950
in the Gaussian case, you are
able to analytically compute

00:39:34.950 --> 00:39:37.110
the true score.

00:39:37.110 --> 00:39:42.030
So what we did is we took
the true data distribution,

00:39:42.030 --> 00:39:44.230
and we noised it.

00:39:44.230 --> 00:39:48.870
And what we showed
was that optimizing

00:39:48.870 --> 00:39:51.610
for the loss that
is not tractable

00:39:51.610 --> 00:39:56.530
is the same as optimizing
for loss that is tractable.

00:39:56.530 --> 00:40:02.570
So in other words, you are
able to estimate the score

00:40:02.570 --> 00:40:06.690
of a noisy data distribution.

00:40:06.690 --> 00:40:12.010
But it is still not equal to
pdata, because what you want

00:40:12.010 --> 00:40:12.630
is pdata.

00:40:12.630 --> 00:40:16.690
You don't want the
noisy data distribution.

00:40:16.690 --> 00:40:20.575
So it's only something
that is going

00:40:20.575 --> 00:40:21.950
in the direction
of what we want,

00:40:21.950 --> 00:40:25.530
but it's not exactly
what we want.

00:40:25.530 --> 00:40:31.210
So let's see what this
q sigma looks like.

00:40:31.210 --> 00:40:36.970
So what we want, again, is
to go from a starting point

00:40:36.970 --> 00:40:40.050
towards the data distribution.

00:40:40.050 --> 00:40:42.370
But here we do not have
the score of the true data

00:40:42.370 --> 00:40:44.090
distribution.

00:40:44.090 --> 00:40:49.680
So how about if we take
a noisy distribution

00:40:49.680 --> 00:40:55.280
that we can compute where
sigma is very, very small.

00:40:55.280 --> 00:40:57.060
So when sigma is
very, very small,

00:40:57.060 --> 00:41:00.280
q sigma is very close
to pdata distribution,

00:41:00.280 --> 00:41:04.320
because we noise it
just a little bit.

00:41:04.320 --> 00:41:09.360
Well, the problem is that if
we take q sigma with sigma

00:41:09.360 --> 00:41:13.680
that is very small,
then we'll just

00:41:13.680 --> 00:41:18.860
have poor estimations of the
score in low-density regions.

00:41:22.120 --> 00:41:25.240
Do you see why?

00:41:25.240 --> 00:41:27.540
So I'm probably going
to just explain this.

00:41:27.540 --> 00:41:29.840
So the good thing is
it's already there.

00:41:29.840 --> 00:41:37.200
So I'm just going to fall back
on the definition of the loss

00:41:37.200 --> 00:41:37.780
here.

00:41:42.280 --> 00:41:45.440
So this is the loss
that we're optimizing.

00:41:45.440 --> 00:41:51.030
So what that means
is that we want

00:41:51.030 --> 00:41:55.670
to optimize on the loss
that is around the distance

00:41:55.670 --> 00:42:01.390
between the predicted
score and the score

00:42:01.390 --> 00:42:17.430
of the noisy distribution
q, which is equal to this.

00:42:17.430 --> 00:42:23.350
So you're weighting your
loss function with respect

00:42:23.350 --> 00:42:32.230
to how likely your points
x tilde are to appear.

00:42:32.230 --> 00:42:40.270
So in other words, if x tilde
is not likely to appear,

00:42:40.270 --> 00:42:44.800
then the fact that it is
off from the true value

00:42:44.800 --> 00:42:47.820
is not something that is
going to be materially

00:42:47.820 --> 00:42:50.300
noticeable in the loss.

00:42:50.300 --> 00:42:54.140
So the model will not
really be incentivized

00:42:54.140 --> 00:42:59.420
to make predictions
accurate for x tilde

00:42:59.420 --> 00:43:03.300
that are not likely to appear.

00:43:03.300 --> 00:43:05.240
OK.

00:43:05.240 --> 00:43:06.800
So does this part make sense?

00:43:06.800 --> 00:43:10.660
So what I'm saying
here is we are

00:43:10.660 --> 00:43:16.620
able to estimate the score of
this noisy data distribution,

00:43:16.620 --> 00:43:20.500
but we're not able to
estimate it accurately

00:43:20.500 --> 00:43:24.900
in places of the
space that are not

00:43:24.900 --> 00:43:28.740
likely to appear under q sigma.

00:43:28.740 --> 00:43:31.940
That's what I'm saying.

00:43:31.940 --> 00:43:35.340
And the problem
with that is if you

00:43:35.340 --> 00:43:37.540
sample from a
distribution that is

00:43:37.540 --> 00:43:41.020
easy to sample from, for
instance, a Gaussian,

00:43:41.020 --> 00:43:47.050
well, most of the time you're
in regions that are low density.

00:43:47.050 --> 00:43:53.090
So if you use the score
of a noisy distribution

00:43:53.090 --> 00:43:55.050
with a small noise,
then you will not

00:43:55.050 --> 00:43:57.250
have estimations
of that score that

00:43:57.250 --> 00:44:00.230
will be accurate in those
low density regions.

00:44:00.230 --> 00:44:04.370
So you will not be
to accurately go

00:44:04.370 --> 00:44:07.810
towards the
high-density regions.

00:44:07.810 --> 00:44:16.770
So in other words,
I'm going just

00:44:16.770 --> 00:44:25.090
to explain this a bit more
on an illustration here.

00:44:25.090 --> 00:44:31.970
So it's as if we were saying
that we have our complicated

00:44:31.970 --> 00:44:35.750
data distribution here that we
have maybe noise a little bit.

00:44:35.750 --> 00:44:38.450
So it's like this.

00:44:38.450 --> 00:44:45.710
So this one is pdata,
and this one is q sigma.

00:44:45.710 --> 00:44:48.030
So what I'm saying
here is we are

00:44:48.030 --> 00:44:54.750
able to compute the
score of this q sigma,

00:44:54.750 --> 00:44:56.590
but we're not able
to estimate it

00:44:56.590 --> 00:45:01.310
accurately in spaces of low
density, so for instance, here.

00:45:01.310 --> 00:45:06.030
So what that means is if
I sample an observation

00:45:06.030 --> 00:45:11.590
from an easy distribution that
is in a low-density region,

00:45:11.590 --> 00:45:15.230
then when I'm going
to use the score

00:45:15.230 --> 00:45:21.403
to get closer to that
region, those estimates

00:45:21.403 --> 00:45:24.990
they may be super off.

00:45:24.990 --> 00:45:30.710
So I won't be able to
come towards where I want.

00:45:30.710 --> 00:45:34.050
And that is the problem with
a small amount of noise.

00:45:34.050 --> 00:45:39.480
So what do you do, well, you can
tell me just increase the noise,

00:45:39.480 --> 00:45:40.420
then.

00:45:40.420 --> 00:45:43.220
Because if you
increase the noise,

00:45:43.220 --> 00:45:48.860
then if you add
enough noise, like,

00:45:48.860 --> 00:46:02.300
let's suppose q sigma with sigma
big, then that point in space

00:46:02.300 --> 00:46:04.900
will be covered with
a high enough density

00:46:04.900 --> 00:46:09.580
such that you can have
more accurate estimations

00:46:09.580 --> 00:46:12.160
of the score of the
noisy distribution.

00:46:12.160 --> 00:46:14.380
But the problem is
that now q sigma is

00:46:14.380 --> 00:46:18.460
too different from your p data.

00:46:18.460 --> 00:46:20.260
So in other terms,
what you're learning

00:46:20.260 --> 00:46:21.720
is not exactly what you want.

00:46:21.720 --> 00:46:24.860
It's like something
that's different.

00:46:24.860 --> 00:46:27.740
So you have this trade-off.

00:46:27.740 --> 00:46:32.280
If you have too little
noise, then it's good.

00:46:32.280 --> 00:46:35.500
What you're trying to learn is
very close to what you actually

00:46:35.500 --> 00:46:38.710
want to learn, which a the score
of the true data distribution.

00:46:38.710 --> 00:46:41.730
But the problem is you do
not have good estimates

00:46:41.730 --> 00:46:44.490
in low-density regions.

00:46:44.490 --> 00:46:49.370
But at the same time, if you
have too high of a noise,

00:46:49.370 --> 00:46:52.730
well, the distribution now is
too different from the true data

00:46:52.730 --> 00:46:53.710
distribution.

00:46:53.710 --> 00:46:59.130
But you now can have
good estimations.

00:46:59.130 --> 00:47:03.050
So it's a little bit
like the bias variance

00:47:03.050 --> 00:47:05.490
trade-off in a sense.

00:47:05.490 --> 00:47:08.410
You just don't have
both at the same time

00:47:08.410 --> 00:47:13.910
in this case, which is making
the situation complicated.

00:47:13.910 --> 00:47:14.410
Yeah.

00:47:14.410 --> 00:47:17.796
[AUDIO OUT]

00:47:23.570 --> 00:47:26.530
So the question is, if we
configure the Langevin sampling

00:47:26.530 --> 00:47:31.990
in a way that can work,
then we can make this work?

00:47:31.990 --> 00:47:33.350
So the answer is yes.

00:47:33.350 --> 00:47:36.520
So it's the next part.

00:47:36.520 --> 00:47:39.160
And we're going to see
how we can do that.

00:47:39.160 --> 00:47:40.660
But yeah, that's
a good intuition.

00:47:40.660 --> 00:47:44.124
[AUDIO OUT]

00:47:53.520 --> 00:47:57.800
So the question is, why is
it poor of an estimation

00:47:57.800 --> 00:48:00.480
in low-density scores?

00:48:00.480 --> 00:48:03.060
This is true.

00:48:03.060 --> 00:48:04.460
It's correct, this one.

00:48:04.460 --> 00:48:08.220
Not this one actually, the
tractable one is the true one.

00:48:08.220 --> 00:48:13.680
But the only problem is that
the tractability of your loss

00:48:13.680 --> 00:48:18.840
function is also a function
of how you configured it

00:48:18.840 --> 00:48:20.340
in terms of the expectation.

00:48:20.340 --> 00:48:24.600
So the expectation
here is over q sigma,

00:48:24.600 --> 00:48:31.200
meaning that if you have an x
tilde, which is in low-density

00:48:31.200 --> 00:48:35.380
regions, then it's just not
going to have a high weight

00:48:35.380 --> 00:48:40.980
loss, which is just not
incentivizing your model

00:48:40.980 --> 00:48:43.580
to actually properly learn it.

00:48:43.580 --> 00:48:47.680
So in a sense, it's a
loss-weighting problem.

00:48:47.680 --> 00:48:50.780
[AUDIO OUT]

00:48:50.780 --> 00:48:52.640
So the predicted score
will be inaccurate.

00:48:52.640 --> 00:48:53.420
Yes.

00:48:53.420 --> 00:48:53.920
Yeah.

00:48:53.920 --> 00:48:57.408
[AUDIO OUT]

00:49:07.740 --> 00:49:08.240
Yeah.

00:49:08.240 --> 00:49:12.740
So the question is, how about
the regularity conditions

00:49:12.740 --> 00:49:14.080
of the function?

00:49:14.080 --> 00:49:16.818
So here let's assume that
this is not a problem.

00:49:16.818 --> 00:49:18.360
Here let's assume
it's not a problem.

00:49:18.360 --> 00:49:22.220
But you're right that, for
instance, for sigma very small

00:49:22.220 --> 00:49:24.740
you have indeed very spiky ones.

00:49:24.740 --> 00:49:26.420
This is the correct intuition.

00:49:26.420 --> 00:49:26.960
So yeah.

00:49:26.960 --> 00:49:28.700
[AUDIO OUT]

00:49:28.700 --> 00:49:29.640
Yes, exactly.

00:49:29.640 --> 00:49:31.260
So high noise smoothens it.

00:49:31.260 --> 00:49:35.610
Low noise produces
some spikiness

00:49:35.610 --> 00:49:38.630
around those
training data points.

00:49:41.730 --> 00:49:42.230
OK, cool.

00:49:42.230 --> 00:49:44.313
I think this is probably
the most challenging part

00:49:44.313 --> 00:49:44.990
of the lecture.

00:49:44.990 --> 00:49:46.690
So yeah, hope you
got the intuition

00:49:46.690 --> 00:49:50.210
as to why that is a problem.

00:49:50.210 --> 00:49:52.350
So what are we going to do?

00:49:52.350 --> 00:49:55.130
And you had the right
intuition in terms

00:49:55.130 --> 00:49:59.090
of making some tweaks to
the Langevin sampling.

00:49:59.090 --> 00:50:03.130
So what we're going
to do is now we're not

00:50:03.130 --> 00:50:07.010
going to learn the score
of one distribution.

00:50:07.010 --> 00:50:11.130
We're going to learn the
score of several distributions

00:50:11.130 --> 00:50:14.770
of several noise levels.

00:50:14.770 --> 00:50:17.950
So instead of
predicting s theta of x,

00:50:17.950 --> 00:50:22.570
we're going to predict s theta
of x where you're in space

00:50:22.570 --> 00:50:26.850
and sigma your noise level.

00:50:26.850 --> 00:50:35.280
And the idea here is to consider
several distributions, one

00:50:35.280 --> 00:50:37.965
with a small amount of noise,
then bigger, then bigger, then

00:50:37.965 --> 00:50:39.320
bigger.

00:50:39.320 --> 00:50:45.080
And the way you're going to use
those different score estimates

00:50:45.080 --> 00:50:48.560
is at the very beginning--

00:50:48.560 --> 00:50:54.000
so if I show you my
beautiful charts here.

00:50:54.000 --> 00:50:56.880
At the very beginning,
what you care

00:50:56.880 --> 00:51:01.200
about is knowing
roughly where to go,

00:51:01.200 --> 00:51:03.520
where to go in space, roughly.

00:51:03.520 --> 00:51:09.000
So here it's fine if you have
the score of a distribution that

00:51:09.000 --> 00:51:13.520
is quite approximate from
the true one, as long

00:51:13.520 --> 00:51:16.440
as you know roughly
where you need to go.

00:51:16.440 --> 00:51:20.120
So the idea is
here, you will use

00:51:20.120 --> 00:51:24.800
the score of a distribution
that has been very noisy.

00:51:24.800 --> 00:51:31.030
And let's say you're going
to go somewhere here.

00:51:31.030 --> 00:51:32.750
And then at that
point, you're going

00:51:32.750 --> 00:51:36.150
to use the score
of a distribution

00:51:36.150 --> 00:51:39.310
with less noise, because maybe
that distribution with less

00:51:39.310 --> 00:51:44.070
noise covers that region.

00:51:44.070 --> 00:51:47.510
And now you know maybe
a little bit better

00:51:47.510 --> 00:51:50.430
where the actual high-density
regions of the true data

00:51:50.430 --> 00:51:52.230
distribution is.

00:51:52.230 --> 00:51:57.070
And you're going to go again
with lower amount of noise

00:51:57.070 --> 00:52:02.750
again and again and
again until coming here.

00:52:02.750 --> 00:52:04.470
So that is the idea.

00:52:04.470 --> 00:52:08.830
You're not going to only rely on
one kind of noisy distribution

00:52:08.830 --> 00:52:11.310
with a small amount
of noise or one

00:52:11.310 --> 00:52:13.150
with a higher amount of noise.

00:52:13.150 --> 00:52:15.870
You're going to actually
rely on all of them

00:52:15.870 --> 00:52:19.770
in a way that will make
this problem easy for you.

00:52:19.770 --> 00:52:22.110
So at the very beginning,
what you care about

00:52:22.110 --> 00:52:24.230
is roughly where to go.

00:52:24.230 --> 00:52:26.910
And then the more
you get closer,

00:52:26.910 --> 00:52:32.210
you care more and more
about the little details.

00:52:32.210 --> 00:52:36.370
And these methods is
something that you

00:52:36.370 --> 00:52:40.530
will recognize with the
following name, so NCSN, Noise

00:52:40.530 --> 00:52:43.250
Conditional Score Network.

00:52:43.250 --> 00:52:47.850
So is this framework of learning
scores of different noise

00:52:47.850 --> 00:52:50.130
levels.

00:52:50.130 --> 00:52:53.350
So I just want to reiterate
what I drew on the blackboard.

00:52:53.350 --> 00:52:58.290
So let's suppose you sample an
observation from a distribution

00:52:58.290 --> 00:53:01.690
that is easy to sample from that
is far from your actual data

00:53:01.690 --> 00:53:03.130
distribution.

00:53:03.130 --> 00:53:05.770
So what you do is
you will actually

00:53:05.770 --> 00:53:11.970
rely on the predicted score from
a noised data distribution that

00:53:11.970 --> 00:53:14.050
is very noisy.

00:53:14.050 --> 00:53:19.170
And you're going
to go step by step

00:53:19.170 --> 00:53:22.570
by relying on scores
from distribution that

00:53:22.570 --> 00:53:29.560
are less and less noise until
coming to your last point, which

00:53:29.560 --> 00:53:33.560
is something that hopefully will
be very, very close to the pdata

00:53:33.560 --> 00:53:36.240
distribution, which is
going to be a noise data

00:53:36.240 --> 00:53:38.500
distribution with
a very low sigma.

00:53:41.900 --> 00:53:42.400
OK.

00:53:42.400 --> 00:53:44.060
So does that make sense?

00:53:47.820 --> 00:53:48.320
Yeah?

00:53:50.800 --> 00:53:51.300
OK.

00:53:51.300 --> 00:53:53.880
Cool

00:53:53.880 --> 00:53:59.400
So back to your intuition
on the Langevin sampling.

00:53:59.400 --> 00:54:01.800
So your intuition
was 100% correct.

00:54:01.800 --> 00:54:07.560
This way of sampling things that
I explained with varying noise

00:54:07.560 --> 00:54:13.960
levels is actually called
annealed Langevin dynamics.

00:54:13.960 --> 00:54:18.920
And it's this idea of starting
with a high amount of noise

00:54:18.920 --> 00:54:23.400
and decreasing the
noise progressively.

00:54:23.400 --> 00:54:28.490
So you will also see that
in the literature as ALD.

00:54:28.490 --> 00:54:28.990
Yeah.

00:54:28.990 --> 00:54:32.466
[AUDIO OUT]

00:54:36.430 --> 00:54:36.930
Yeah.

00:54:36.930 --> 00:54:40.385
[AUDIO OUT]

00:54:48.750 --> 00:54:51.530
So the question is, what is
exactly the trade-off here?

00:54:51.530 --> 00:54:55.170
So the idea is with a
high amount of noise,

00:54:55.170 --> 00:54:59.430
if you add a high
enough amount of noise,

00:54:59.430 --> 00:55:04.550
then previous low-density
regions will be covered

00:55:04.550 --> 00:55:06.670
by the noisy distribution.

00:55:06.670 --> 00:55:10.390
And if you add
enough noise, like,

00:55:10.390 --> 00:55:13.770
with a very high amount of
sigma, then you will cover it.

00:55:13.770 --> 00:55:15.750
The only problem is--

00:55:15.750 --> 00:55:20.930
so you can learn the score of
that very noisy distribution.

00:55:20.930 --> 00:55:23.780
But the problem is that the true
score of that noisy distribution

00:55:23.780 --> 00:55:27.458
is going to be far from the
score of the data distribution.

00:55:27.458 --> 00:55:28.500
So that is the trade-off.

00:55:28.500 --> 00:55:31.780
[AUDIO OUT]

00:55:37.520 --> 00:55:38.020
Right.

00:55:38.020 --> 00:55:40.540
So the direction that
it will be pointing

00:55:40.540 --> 00:55:45.140
will be roughly where you
want to go, but not exactly.

00:55:45.140 --> 00:55:48.100
But the idea here is that
the very beginning you

00:55:48.100 --> 00:55:49.160
don't really care.

00:55:49.160 --> 00:55:51.880
So for instance, if
from here from Stanford,

00:55:51.880 --> 00:55:56.700
I want to go to New York, I know
roughly I want to go this way.

00:55:56.700 --> 00:55:57.840
And then I go this way.

00:55:57.840 --> 00:55:59.580
And then the more I
get closer, the more

00:55:59.580 --> 00:56:02.720
I want to refine exactly
where New York is.

00:56:02.720 --> 00:56:03.220
Yeah.

00:56:06.140 --> 00:56:08.220
OK, cool.

00:56:08.220 --> 00:56:10.240
There is something
else I wanted to say.

00:56:13.960 --> 00:56:14.460
OK.

00:56:14.460 --> 00:56:17.160
So there's something
else I wanted to say,

00:56:17.160 --> 00:56:19.860
which I think we'll
cover a little bit later.

00:56:19.860 --> 00:56:22.650
But first, we're
going to cover how

00:56:22.650 --> 00:56:25.250
we're going to sample
new observations

00:56:25.250 --> 00:56:28.930
using this annealed
Langevin dynamics.

00:56:28.930 --> 00:56:32.370
So here what you
would do is sample

00:56:32.370 --> 00:56:38.250
some noise from
very noisy Gaussian

00:56:38.250 --> 00:56:40.890
with a high amount of noise.

00:56:40.890 --> 00:56:46.730
And what you're going to do
is for every noise level,

00:56:46.730 --> 00:56:50.810
you're going to run
your Langevin sampling.

00:56:50.810 --> 00:56:54.370
And this is the formula that we
saw at the very beginning, which

00:56:54.370 --> 00:56:59.950
is you add the point that you're
at with the predicted score,

00:56:59.950 --> 00:57:02.570
which is where you
want to go, and then

00:57:02.570 --> 00:57:08.170
some amount of noise, which
allows for a bit of diversity.

00:57:08.170 --> 00:57:11.450
And once you do that
for all noise levels

00:57:11.450 --> 00:57:15.490
until coming to a very
small noise level,

00:57:15.490 --> 00:57:20.090
then you end up with
the actual image.

00:57:23.110 --> 00:57:27.110
So one thing that I want
to say regarding the score,

00:57:27.110 --> 00:57:32.070
you can think of the
score as being a compass.

00:57:32.070 --> 00:57:33.750
Now, when you are in
the forest and you

00:57:33.750 --> 00:57:36.710
want to see where
the city is, just

00:57:36.710 --> 00:57:39.890
follow the direction
of the compass.

00:57:39.890 --> 00:57:42.470
It's the same idea here.

00:57:42.470 --> 00:57:45.510
So we're in the space,
space of images.

00:57:45.510 --> 00:57:50.070
A lot of these regions
of space are images that

00:57:50.070 --> 00:57:51.950
are actually not real images.

00:57:51.950 --> 00:57:56.510
But what you want is
to go towards regions

00:57:56.510 --> 00:58:02.750
of high density with respect to
your target data distribution.

00:58:02.750 --> 00:58:05.550
So it's as if you were
following a compass

00:58:05.550 --> 00:58:07.050
to come at your destination.

00:58:09.990 --> 00:58:10.650
OK, cool.

00:58:10.650 --> 00:58:13.610
So last time-- you
had a question?

00:58:13.610 --> 00:58:17.026
[AUDIO OUT]

00:58:22.860 --> 00:58:23.560
Oh, yeah.

00:58:23.560 --> 00:58:25.740
So the question is
data distribution

00:58:25.740 --> 00:58:27.200
can be any data distribution?

00:58:27.200 --> 00:58:28.120
Yes, yes.

00:58:30.960 --> 00:58:31.460
OK.

00:58:31.460 --> 00:58:38.060
So now what I want to tell you
is how what I'm telling you

00:58:38.060 --> 00:58:42.860
now here relates to
what we saw last week.

00:58:42.860 --> 00:58:50.140
So if you remember, DDPM was
all about taking a clean image

00:58:50.140 --> 00:58:52.340
and then noising it.

00:58:52.340 --> 00:58:56.740
So if you remember, we
had these coefficients.

00:58:56.740 --> 00:58:59.380
And for instance, in this
case, the noised image

00:58:59.380 --> 00:59:03.540
was the square root of alpha
t bar times your clean image

00:59:03.540 --> 00:59:09.900
plus square root of 1 minus
alpha t bar times the noise.

00:59:09.900 --> 00:59:15.170
What I told you here
also involves noise.

00:59:15.170 --> 00:59:18.430
So a natural question
that you may ask is,

00:59:18.430 --> 00:59:24.370
how does the score relate
to the noise prediction task

00:59:24.370 --> 00:59:27.130
that we saw last week?

00:59:27.130 --> 00:59:34.090
Well, the forward
process of xt given x0,

00:59:34.090 --> 00:59:36.850
you just see that it is a
normal distribution centered

00:59:36.850 --> 00:59:42.010
on the square root of
alpha t bar times x0.

00:59:42.010 --> 00:59:49.970
And you actually realize that
the score of that distribution

00:59:49.970 --> 00:59:57.190
is equal to minus the noise that
you added over some coefficient.

01:00:00.050 --> 01:00:03.230
So in other words,
these two quantities,

01:00:03.230 --> 01:00:05.130
so the score meaning
where you should

01:00:05.130 --> 01:00:09.250
go to end up where
the clean images are

01:00:09.250 --> 01:00:13.050
and the noise that you
added, are actually

01:00:13.050 --> 01:00:16.400
two quantities that are linked.

01:00:16.400 --> 01:00:18.200
And they are linked
such that the score

01:00:18.200 --> 01:00:21.900
is equal to minus the
noise that you added.

01:00:21.900 --> 01:00:24.280
And that makes sense.

01:00:24.280 --> 01:00:26.440
So why does that make sense?

01:00:26.440 --> 01:00:32.120
So I'm just going
to clean that part.

01:00:32.120 --> 01:00:32.720
Whoops.

01:00:38.960 --> 01:00:40.500
So why does that make sense?

01:00:43.440 --> 01:00:46.920
So here if you have
your clean image

01:00:46.920 --> 01:00:52.400
and then if you noised,
so it's as if you added

01:00:52.400 --> 01:00:58.540
some amount of something noise.

01:00:58.540 --> 01:01:04.200
But then if you want to go
back to high-density regions

01:01:04.200 --> 01:01:07.240
it's basically your score.

01:01:07.240 --> 01:01:10.980
So it makes sense that the two
are in opposite directions.

01:01:13.500 --> 01:01:15.960
So by the way, does that make
sense that it is the formula?

01:01:15.960 --> 01:01:17.980
So maybe I can also derive it.

01:01:17.980 --> 01:01:27.980
So remember that the score
of a normal distribution

01:01:27.980 --> 01:01:37.040
is equal to minus x minus
the mean over the variance.

01:01:37.040 --> 01:01:46.220
So in this case, we have--

01:01:46.220 --> 01:01:47.360
so x, what is x?

01:01:47.360 --> 01:01:49.540
It's a noisy image.

01:01:49.540 --> 01:01:53.040
And the noisy image has
the following formula.

01:01:53.040 --> 01:01:56.420
So noisy image is
the square root

01:01:56.420 --> 01:01:59.900
of alpha t bar times
x0 plus square root

01:01:59.900 --> 01:02:03.580
of 1 minus alpha t
bar times epsilon.

01:02:03.580 --> 01:02:07.720
You subtract the mean of
the normal distribution,

01:02:07.720 --> 01:02:13.530
which is this square root
of alpha t bar times x0.

01:02:13.530 --> 01:02:16.610
So this gives you minus.

01:02:16.610 --> 01:02:23.930
And then this is square root
of 1 minus alpha t bar epsilon

01:02:23.930 --> 01:02:28.350
over your variance, which
is 1 minus alpha t bar.

01:02:32.250 --> 01:02:35.430
Which is exactly what
you have on the slides.

01:02:41.930 --> 01:02:42.870
Does that make sense?

01:02:45.670 --> 01:02:46.170
OK.

01:02:46.170 --> 01:02:46.670
Cool.

01:02:46.670 --> 01:02:54.970
So this is just to give you a
parallel between NCSN and DDPM.

01:02:54.970 --> 01:02:59.970
So these two methods are
just two different ways

01:02:59.970 --> 01:03:05.470
of noising your sample at the
end of the day, because one

01:03:05.470 --> 01:03:11.120
is taking some weighted average
between the clean sample

01:03:11.120 --> 01:03:14.720
x0 and the noise, and the
other one is just adding noise

01:03:14.720 --> 01:03:16.360
with just sigma.

01:03:16.360 --> 01:03:21.840
So that is the reason
why this, so DDPM, you

01:03:21.840 --> 01:03:27.400
will see it be characterized
as being variance preserving.

01:03:27.400 --> 01:03:29.880
Because if you take
the variance of xt

01:03:29.880 --> 01:03:34.280
is going to be
roughly equal to 1.

01:03:34.280 --> 01:03:38.620
And this is assuming that
your x0 are normalized.

01:03:38.620 --> 01:03:40.960
They have a mean of
0 and variance of 1.

01:03:40.960 --> 01:03:42.760
So if you take the
variance of xt,

01:03:42.760 --> 01:03:48.600
it's roughly alpha t bar plus--
so square root of alpha t bar

01:03:48.600 --> 01:03:53.620
squared plus square root of
1 minus alpha t bar squared,

01:03:53.620 --> 01:03:56.360
which is equal to 1 roughly.

01:03:56.360 --> 01:04:02.360
And this formulation,
the NCSN one

01:04:02.360 --> 01:04:08.830
is going to be mentioned as
being variants exploding.

01:04:08.830 --> 01:04:12.130
Oh, I have to go way
back in the slides.

01:04:12.130 --> 01:04:16.150
This one is going to be variance
exploding because you're going

01:04:16.150 --> 01:04:18.210
to add more and more noise.

01:04:18.210 --> 01:04:21.110
And the noise is going
to just increase a lot.

01:04:21.110 --> 01:04:23.010
So it's not bounded by 1.

01:04:27.230 --> 01:04:27.750
OK.

01:04:27.750 --> 01:04:29.750
Cool.

01:04:29.750 --> 01:04:35.390
So, so far what
we've seen is noising

01:04:35.390 --> 01:04:42.750
images using a discrete
set of noise increments.

01:04:42.750 --> 01:04:50.990
But one could argue that these
discretization is arbitrary.

01:04:50.990 --> 01:04:54.950
It is something that you need
to decide before starting

01:04:54.950 --> 01:04:56.910
your training process.

01:04:56.910 --> 01:05:02.390
So one idea is to actually
consider continuous evolution

01:05:02.390 --> 01:05:04.810
instead of discrete one.

01:05:04.810 --> 01:05:07.410
So the reason why you would
be interested in doing that

01:05:07.410 --> 01:05:11.790
is because we also
have a field of math,

01:05:11.790 --> 01:05:15.250
which is in differential
equations that

01:05:15.250 --> 01:05:19.890
have a bunch of very
convenient toolkits to solve

01:05:19.890 --> 01:05:21.690
these kinds of equations.

01:05:21.690 --> 01:05:25.730
So one idea is to
actually start from what

01:05:25.730 --> 01:05:32.850
we saw in DDPM and NCSN and
try to see how they would be

01:05:32.850 --> 01:05:36.650
like in the continuous world.

01:05:36.650 --> 01:05:41.010
So in order to do
that, we first need

01:05:41.010 --> 01:05:45.810
to have an equivalent
of noise increments.

01:05:45.810 --> 01:05:51.250
Because up until now, the way
we added noise to clean images

01:05:51.250 --> 01:05:55.550
was via sampling from a
Gaussian distribution.

01:05:55.550 --> 01:05:58.090
So it's like normal
distribution of mean 0

01:05:58.090 --> 01:06:01.530
and covariance
matrix, the identity,

01:06:01.530 --> 01:06:04.880
and then just adding
noise like this.

01:06:04.880 --> 01:06:11.480
But what you want is to have a
continuous equivalent of having

01:06:11.480 --> 01:06:13.600
these noise increments.

01:06:13.600 --> 01:06:18.560
And this is where the
Wiener process comes in.

01:06:18.560 --> 01:06:23.100
So who has heard of the
Wiener process before?

01:06:23.100 --> 01:06:23.600
Yeah.

01:06:23.600 --> 01:06:24.640
No one?

01:06:24.640 --> 01:06:25.220
Maybe one.

01:06:25.220 --> 01:06:25.880
OK.

01:06:25.880 --> 01:06:31.300
So the Wiener process is a
type of stochastic process.

01:06:31.300 --> 01:06:36.400
So stochastic process is a
family of random variables

01:06:36.400 --> 01:06:41.480
that are indexed by some
index t, let's say, the time.

01:06:41.480 --> 01:06:45.320
And a Wiener process
is a stochastic process

01:06:45.320 --> 01:06:49.520
that follows the
following properties.

01:06:49.520 --> 01:06:55.120
So we have W0 by definition
that is equal to 0.

01:06:55.120 --> 01:07:03.790
And the difference between Wt
and Ws, which is an increment,

01:07:03.790 --> 01:07:06.870
follows a normal
distribution of mean 0

01:07:06.870 --> 01:07:14.250
and variance t minus s times
I. And these increments,

01:07:14.250 --> 01:07:16.470
so these differences
are actually

01:07:16.470 --> 01:07:20.830
independent from one another.

01:07:20.830 --> 01:07:24.590
So you can think of the
equivalent of a random walk.

01:07:24.590 --> 01:07:26.710
So you walk one
here and then one

01:07:26.710 --> 01:07:32.870
there but in the
continuous world.

01:07:32.870 --> 01:07:37.510
So one thing that I want to
say is that what is independent

01:07:37.510 --> 01:07:43.230
is the increments and not the
value of the ability itself.

01:07:43.230 --> 01:07:49.430
Because you can imagine that
Wt will depend on Wt minus 1

01:07:49.430 --> 01:07:50.170
and so on.

01:07:50.170 --> 01:07:52.510
So what we're saying is
that the increments are

01:07:52.510 --> 01:07:56.110
independent from one another.

01:07:56.110 --> 01:07:59.700
So with that in
mind, what we can do

01:07:59.700 --> 01:08:07.140
is to derive a continuous
formulation of DDPM and NCSN.

01:08:07.140 --> 01:08:10.660
So in the interest of
time, what I'm going to do

01:08:10.660 --> 01:08:17.000
is to only derive the DDPM case.

01:08:21.380 --> 01:08:25.460
So what we're saying
is we're starting

01:08:25.460 --> 01:08:29.060
from the update
formula that adds noise

01:08:29.060 --> 01:08:31.899
to a less noisy image.

01:08:31.899 --> 01:08:36.300
And what we want is to
obtain a continuous way

01:08:36.300 --> 01:08:37.920
of expressing that.

01:08:37.920 --> 01:08:40.500
So I'm just going to rewrite
what is on the slide.

01:08:40.500 --> 01:08:48.020
So xt is equal to square root
of 1 minus beta of xt minus 1

01:08:48.020 --> 01:08:51.920
plus square root of
beta times epsilon.

01:08:51.920 --> 01:08:55.460
So if you remember, beta
is your noise schedule.

01:08:55.460 --> 01:09:00.490
It's the amount of noise that
you add at each time step.

01:09:00.490 --> 01:09:10.130
And so what you want is to have
an expression of a differential

01:09:10.130 --> 01:09:16.890
evolution in x as a
function of everything else.

01:09:16.890 --> 01:09:20.370
So what I'm going to do
is subtract that quantity

01:09:20.370 --> 01:09:26.830
with xt minus 1 And then
have t and t minus 1

01:09:26.830 --> 01:09:28.770
be very, very
small so that I can

01:09:28.770 --> 01:09:31.490
consider this to be continuous.

01:09:31.490 --> 01:09:35.370
So what I'm going to do
here is just some math.

01:09:35.370 --> 01:09:42.850
So I'm taking xt and I'm
subtracting xt minus 1.

01:09:42.850 --> 01:09:50.370
So it's going to be square
root of 1 minus beta minus 1,

01:09:50.370 --> 01:10:01.270
xt minus 1 plus square
root of beta t epsilon.

01:10:01.270 --> 01:10:03.630
So beta t is the
quantity of noise

01:10:03.630 --> 01:10:07.830
that you add between
two discrete steps.

01:10:07.830 --> 01:10:13.550
What I'm going to do
is to define a quantity

01:10:13.550 --> 01:10:17.190
that I'm going to
write beta parenthesis

01:10:17.190 --> 01:10:24.910
t, which is the rate of
noise at time t times

01:10:24.910 --> 01:10:28.470
dt, an increment of time.

01:10:28.470 --> 01:10:30.230
So what this is
saying is when we're

01:10:30.230 --> 01:10:35.530
going to have t10 to 0-- so
this is going to 10 to 0,

01:10:35.530 --> 01:10:38.830
but we're going to have
some rates of noise that's

01:10:38.830 --> 01:10:43.710
going to be nonzero, that's
going to be your schedule.

01:10:43.710 --> 01:10:56.180
So if I pause that, so this
becomes xt minus xt minus 1

01:10:56.180 --> 01:11:13.060
is equal to, so 1 minus
beta t dt minus 1 xt minus 1

01:11:13.060 --> 01:11:18.600
plus square root--

01:11:18.600 --> 01:11:20.700
I'm going to also
replace it here--

01:11:20.700 --> 01:11:24.980
of beta t dt epsilon.

01:11:28.416 --> 01:11:32.680
So far I've just plugged in
the new beta t expression.

01:11:35.260 --> 01:11:42.172
So what we're
going to obtain now

01:11:42.172 --> 01:11:45.120
is actually something that's
going to be quite nice.

01:11:57.170 --> 01:12:05.930
So if we tend t to 0, so if we
consider a continuous evolution,

01:12:05.930 --> 01:12:08.970
xt minus xt minus
1 is also going

01:12:08.970 --> 01:12:13.410
to be a differential evolution.

01:12:13.410 --> 01:12:18.830
So here, I'm going to rewrite xt
minus xt minus 1 as just d of x.

01:12:22.530 --> 01:12:30.570
So when dt tends to 0, you have
Taylor expansion formulas that

01:12:30.570 --> 01:12:34.730
tells you that square root of 1
minus something that tends to 0,

01:12:34.730 --> 01:12:41.450
which is equal to 1 minus
1/2 of that something.

01:12:41.450 --> 01:12:43.850
So this is just
Taylor expansion.

01:12:43.850 --> 01:12:52.520
So here, I'm going to say it's
roughly equal to 1 minus 1/2

01:12:52.520 --> 01:12:57.160
of beta t dt.

01:12:57.160 --> 01:12:59.940
xt minus 1 is roughly
going to be x.

01:13:03.600 --> 01:13:07.640
So this is 1 minus this
minus 1, because you

01:13:07.640 --> 01:13:13.080
have the 1 here, times x plus--

01:13:13.080 --> 01:13:18.400
so you have square
root of beta t.

01:13:18.400 --> 01:13:22.360
But then the Wiener
process that we defined

01:13:22.360 --> 01:13:30.000
is such that a small increment
in the Wiener process

01:13:30.000 --> 01:13:32.960
is actually something
that you can rewrite

01:13:32.960 --> 01:13:36.900
as being square root
of dt times epsilon,

01:13:36.900 --> 01:13:40.800
because it follows a normal
distribution of variance dt

01:13:40.800 --> 01:13:44.280
times I.

01:13:44.280 --> 01:13:50.060
So here, I'm going to recognize
the square root of dt times e.

01:13:50.060 --> 01:13:50.920
That's right.

01:13:50.920 --> 01:13:57.200
Square root of dt times epsilon
as being dW, which is equal--

01:13:57.200 --> 01:14:01.360
so it's as if it was
equal to d of x is equal,

01:14:01.360 --> 01:14:11.220
so this is going to cancel out,
1/2 of beta x dt plus square

01:14:11.220 --> 01:14:13.456
root of B dW.

01:14:16.220 --> 01:14:17.740
And now you may
be wondering, why

01:14:17.740 --> 01:14:19.940
am I writing all
these equations?

01:14:19.940 --> 01:14:21.860
Why are we doing this?

01:14:21.860 --> 01:14:26.540
Well, you're going to see
that when we rewrite this,

01:14:26.540 --> 01:14:33.180
for instance, for DDPM and
then for NCSN, what we realize

01:14:33.180 --> 01:14:39.100
is that we have an expression
of dx equal to something times

01:14:39.100 --> 01:14:45.980
dt plus something times dW.

01:14:45.980 --> 01:14:50.410
So these two terms have
very specific names.

01:14:50.410 --> 01:14:52.610
So one is called the drift.

01:14:52.610 --> 01:14:54.430
So this quantity is
called the drift.

01:14:54.430 --> 01:14:56.450
So it's deterministic.

01:14:56.450 --> 01:14:59.070
And this one is
called the diffusion,

01:14:59.070 --> 01:15:02.210
which is what is stochastic.

01:15:02.210 --> 01:15:06.450
And you actually have a
nice intuition about it

01:15:06.450 --> 01:15:11.890
when it comes to how
x varies in space.

01:15:11.890 --> 01:15:15.250
So if you suppose x
to start from here

01:15:15.250 --> 01:15:19.250
and you only have
a drift term, what

01:15:19.250 --> 01:15:25.930
you're going to have is just
deterministic evolution using

01:15:25.930 --> 01:15:28.570
f of x and t.

01:15:28.570 --> 01:15:30.410
So you're going
to go from x to x

01:15:30.410 --> 01:15:34.230
plus dx using f of xt times dt.

01:15:34.230 --> 01:15:37.490
And then you're going to repeat
this process again and again

01:15:37.490 --> 01:15:40.450
in a deterministic fashion.

01:15:40.450 --> 01:15:45.130
So this first term
is deterministic.

01:15:45.130 --> 01:15:48.960
Now let's look at what
the second term does.

01:15:48.960 --> 01:15:53.440
So here you start
from a point x,

01:15:53.440 --> 01:16:00.480
and you add some quantity
that is a function of noise,

01:16:00.480 --> 01:16:02.440
Gaussian noise.

01:16:02.440 --> 01:16:05.760
So here it's going to be
something that is noisy, so

01:16:05.760 --> 01:16:08.920
the stochastic part.

01:16:08.920 --> 01:16:13.120
Which means that if you
take the whole expression,

01:16:13.120 --> 01:16:16.840
you're going to end
up with something--

01:16:16.840 --> 01:16:22.120
so one step from
x to x plus dx is

01:16:22.120 --> 01:16:25.880
going to be including
a deterministic step

01:16:25.880 --> 01:16:28.640
and a stochastic step.

01:16:28.640 --> 01:16:34.040
And this is something that
will follow the slides here.

01:16:34.040 --> 01:16:41.080
So all I want to say is that
DDPM and NCSN can actually

01:16:41.080 --> 01:16:45.310
be transcribed from their
discrete formulations

01:16:45.310 --> 01:16:47.630
to a continuous one.

01:16:47.630 --> 01:16:49.510
So when you see
these formulas, just

01:16:49.510 --> 01:16:52.910
know that it is
just us transcribing

01:16:52.910 --> 01:16:54.930
them to be continuous.

01:16:54.930 --> 01:16:56.790
And the reason why
we want to do that

01:16:56.790 --> 01:17:01.870
is because this is going
to be an SDE, so Stochastic

01:17:01.870 --> 01:17:07.070
Differential Equation, for
which the field already

01:17:07.070 --> 01:17:11.990
has a lot of techniques
to solve them.

01:17:11.990 --> 01:17:14.590
And I'm just going
to tell you now

01:17:14.590 --> 01:17:21.630
about why the DDPM
formula makes sense just

01:17:21.630 --> 01:17:23.010
from an intuitive perspective.

01:17:23.010 --> 01:17:28.410
So what we're saying is in a 2D
space, so this is the origin.

01:17:28.410 --> 01:17:30.910
This is 0.

01:17:30.910 --> 01:17:34.750
And then we have a vector x.

01:17:34.750 --> 01:17:38.510
So what we're saying is
that an increment in x

01:17:38.510 --> 01:17:43.590
is going to be negative
with respect to where x is.

01:17:43.590 --> 01:17:47.210
So it's going to be
somewhere like this.

01:17:47.210 --> 01:17:50.090
And then we're going to have
a little bit of noise added

01:17:50.090 --> 01:17:52.830
to it, so maybe like this.

01:17:55.890 --> 01:17:59.030
So in other words
the noising process,

01:17:59.030 --> 01:18:06.930
the forward process is
bringing x closer to 0

01:18:06.930 --> 01:18:11.530
and adding some
noise so that this

01:18:11.530 --> 01:18:16.810
converges to a variance of 1,
which is what DDPM is about.

01:18:16.810 --> 01:18:20.730
Because the DDPM you
take a clean image,

01:18:20.730 --> 01:18:26.690
and then you noise it with
convenience coefficients

01:18:26.690 --> 01:18:31.610
such that after t steps, it
becomes a normal distribution

01:18:31.610 --> 01:18:35.050
of mean 0 and variance 1.

01:18:35.050 --> 01:18:36.770
And this is exactly
what you're also

01:18:36.770 --> 01:18:40.070
seeing by interpreting the
terms of this equation.

01:18:43.560 --> 01:18:44.460
Does that make sense?

01:18:44.460 --> 01:18:47.800
So I just want to tell
you this to just tell you

01:18:47.800 --> 01:18:50.060
that these formulas
are not just formulas.

01:18:50.060 --> 01:18:54.520
They have a meaning if
you think about each term.

01:18:54.520 --> 01:18:59.400
And here this expression
of the continuous evolution

01:18:59.400 --> 01:19:05.000
of the forward process for DDPM
actually makes intuitive sense

01:19:05.000 --> 01:19:06.620
when you're just
writing things down.

01:19:09.520 --> 01:19:12.976
[AUDIO OUT]

01:19:12.976 --> 01:19:15.260
So question is,
why is it useful?

01:19:15.260 --> 01:19:17.120
We're going to see
it exactly now.

01:19:17.120 --> 01:19:20.660
So I'm going to tell
you now what is useful.

01:19:20.660 --> 01:19:24.320
So the reason why
it's useful is 1,

01:19:24.320 --> 01:19:29.920
you're not going to have
to necessarily define

01:19:29.920 --> 01:19:34.625
discretization steps
between each noised images.

01:19:34.625 --> 01:19:36.000
So you're not
going to say, OK, I

01:19:36.000 --> 01:19:38.950
need let's say t steps to do
the whole noising process.

01:19:38.950 --> 01:19:40.670
So that's the number 1.

01:19:40.670 --> 01:19:44.230
And number 2 is there's
a number of results

01:19:44.230 --> 01:19:47.750
in the field of stochastic
differential equations, which

01:19:47.750 --> 01:19:50.790
is essentially what this is.

01:19:50.790 --> 01:19:53.870
There's a number of
results that will allow you

01:19:53.870 --> 01:19:57.870
to solve the reverse process.

01:19:57.870 --> 01:20:03.910
Because what you want is to
be able to denoise images.

01:20:03.910 --> 01:20:06.170
So here what we're doing is
we're just noising images.

01:20:06.170 --> 01:20:08.890
But what we care about
is how to denoise images,

01:20:08.890 --> 01:20:11.150
which is the reverse process.

01:20:11.150 --> 01:20:14.790
And the reason why it
is convenient for us

01:20:14.790 --> 01:20:18.830
to think in the
continuous world is

01:20:18.830 --> 01:20:21.910
for us to be able to
leverage these results.

01:20:21.910 --> 01:20:28.110
And we're going to see these
results in a few minutes.

01:20:28.110 --> 01:20:28.970
OK, cool.

01:20:28.970 --> 01:20:32.230
So here I just want to
say that this drift term

01:20:32.230 --> 01:20:37.720
f and the diffusion g is
the following for DDPM,

01:20:37.720 --> 01:20:41.720
but it is this form for NCSN.

01:20:41.720 --> 01:20:44.040
And you see that there
is no drift term,

01:20:44.040 --> 01:20:47.280
and it is mainly because
we're only adding noise.

01:20:49.980 --> 01:20:57.540
And the idea behind
DDPM and score matching

01:20:57.540 --> 01:21:01.020
is actually something that
was developed in parallel.

01:21:01.020 --> 01:21:05.620
So here what I want to say is
just a historical evolution

01:21:05.620 --> 01:21:08.460
of how things happened
and how we came up

01:21:08.460 --> 01:21:13.060
to that unified view of
describing the forward process

01:21:13.060 --> 01:21:14.720
in order to solve
the reverse process.

01:21:14.720 --> 01:21:19.060
So on the one side, the
diffusion literature

01:21:19.060 --> 01:21:22.340
had this given branch
with a 2015 paper

01:21:22.340 --> 01:21:27.740
just describing how you can
derive a tractable loss in order

01:21:27.740 --> 01:21:30.597
to solve for this,
the diffusion problem.

01:21:30.597 --> 01:21:32.180
And then you have
the DDPM paper which

01:21:32.180 --> 01:21:34.940
came out in 2020
that successfully

01:21:34.940 --> 01:21:37.760
applied that for images.

01:21:37.760 --> 01:21:39.900
That was on the one side.

01:21:39.900 --> 01:21:43.720
And on the other side in
to score matching world,

01:21:43.720 --> 01:21:47.280
you had this principle
of using the score

01:21:47.280 --> 01:21:51.800
to go from a starting point to
the distribution and the fact

01:21:51.800 --> 01:21:56.280
that it alleviated this
intractable normalizing

01:21:56.280 --> 01:21:57.480
constant.

01:21:57.480 --> 01:22:00.720
And then there was a
paper that described

01:22:00.720 --> 01:22:04.920
how to do that in the case
of a noise distribution.

01:22:04.920 --> 01:22:07.480
And then the NCSN
method is actually

01:22:07.480 --> 01:22:11.560
something that came
out with a later paper.

01:22:11.560 --> 01:22:15.400
And there is one
paper that unified

01:22:15.400 --> 01:22:19.960
both of these paradigms, which
is a paper called Score-Based

01:22:19.960 --> 01:22:22.200
Generative Modeling Through
Stochastic Differential

01:22:22.200 --> 01:22:25.560
Equation, which is a paper
I highly recommend reading.

01:22:25.560 --> 01:22:28.720
It's actually written
by folks from Stanford,

01:22:28.720 --> 01:22:34.190
so Yang Song and coauthors.

01:22:34.190 --> 01:22:40.390
And it is from that moment that
the community has seen these two

01:22:40.390 --> 01:22:43.630
paradigms as being unified.

01:22:43.630 --> 01:22:50.550
And this is the way we
have seen this play out.

01:22:50.550 --> 01:22:54.430
So now why did we do
this in the first place?

01:22:54.430 --> 01:23:01.070
So our goal is to
recover clean images.

01:23:01.070 --> 01:23:04.950
So we know how to noise them
through this forward process

01:23:04.950 --> 01:23:09.150
that we now are able to
express in continuous term.

01:23:09.150 --> 01:23:15.230
What we care about is to
denoise into clean images.

01:23:15.230 --> 01:23:23.790
And so here what we're doing
again predicting the score.

01:23:23.790 --> 01:23:27.030
So this score
formulation is just

01:23:27.030 --> 01:23:30.070
a regression on the
conditional score which

01:23:30.070 --> 01:23:35.180
we saw was tractable,
which is, as we saw,

01:23:35.180 --> 01:23:40.140
minus epsilon over
some coefficients.

01:23:40.140 --> 01:23:43.340
And the way you would
train such a model

01:23:43.340 --> 01:23:46.820
is following this
loss function, which

01:23:46.820 --> 01:23:51.340
is an expectation over
clean images that you noise.

01:23:51.340 --> 01:23:56.380
And so what you do is you
sample a clean image and then

01:23:56.380 --> 01:23:58.460
some noise and time step.

01:23:58.460 --> 01:24:01.820
And then what you do is you
obtain your noised image.

01:24:01.820 --> 01:24:05.180
And so here noised image
is just a combination

01:24:05.180 --> 01:24:09.720
of the clean image and the noise
with some alpha and sigma t,

01:24:09.720 --> 01:24:14.740
which can be chosen either in
the DDPM way or another way.

01:24:14.740 --> 01:24:20.260
And what you do is
you predict the score

01:24:20.260 --> 01:24:26.260
that you know is tractable using
your noised image and your time

01:24:26.260 --> 01:24:29.020
step.

01:24:29.020 --> 01:24:35.530
But now you may be
asking, well, why do

01:24:35.530 --> 01:24:37.930
you want to predict the score?

01:24:37.930 --> 01:24:44.570
Because we saw that for
NCSN, we had the score

01:24:44.570 --> 01:24:48.890
that we used in order to sample
new observations from the data

01:24:48.890 --> 01:24:49.550
distribution.

01:24:49.550 --> 01:24:53.770
But we saw in the case
of DDPM that we actually

01:24:53.770 --> 01:24:58.210
predicted the noise to remove.

01:24:58.210 --> 01:25:00.850
So we had these two
different formulations.

01:25:00.850 --> 01:25:03.370
So now the question
that you may ask is,

01:25:03.370 --> 01:25:08.210
why do you want to
focus on the score here?

01:25:08.210 --> 01:25:14.250
And the answer to that is
that the forward process that

01:25:14.250 --> 01:25:22.450
is described by the SDE can be
reversed through a reverse SDE

01:25:22.450 --> 01:25:25.470
that involves the score.

01:25:28.960 --> 01:25:32.120
So I'm just going to
repeat exactly what I said.

01:25:32.120 --> 01:25:35.880
So the forward process, which
is described by the SDE,

01:25:35.880 --> 01:25:40.200
is something that you can
reverse through a reverse SDE

01:25:40.200 --> 01:25:43.080
which involves the score.

01:25:43.080 --> 01:25:46.200
And the reason why the
score is popping up here

01:25:46.200 --> 01:25:50.860
is due to some derivations that
we're not going to go here,

01:25:50.860 --> 01:25:57.220
but this is just a result that
comes from a paper from 1982.

01:25:57.220 --> 01:25:59.560
So it's a result
from the SDE world

01:25:59.560 --> 01:26:01.620
that were just leveraging here.

01:26:04.960 --> 01:26:07.640
So that is one of
the points why we're

01:26:07.640 --> 01:26:09.680
caring about the
continuous formulation

01:26:09.680 --> 01:26:12.920
is because we want to
be able to leverage

01:26:12.920 --> 01:26:16.860
results around these SDEs,
and this is one of them.

01:26:20.560 --> 01:26:25.640
And so now I want to
walk you through what

01:26:25.640 --> 01:26:29.700
that reverse process
equation is about.

01:26:29.700 --> 01:26:36.300
So I told you that the forward
process was composed of a drift

01:26:36.300 --> 01:26:39.940
term and a diffusion term.

01:26:39.940 --> 01:26:43.460
So it was dx equal
to f of x and t times

01:26:43.460 --> 01:26:48.260
dt, which is the drift,
and then plus gt times

01:26:48.260 --> 01:26:52.740
dW, which is the stochastic
term, the diffusion term.

01:26:52.740 --> 01:26:57.900
And what we're saying is that in
order to do the reverse process,

01:26:57.900 --> 01:27:02.660
we actually cannot just
have the same equation.

01:27:02.660 --> 01:27:08.100
We need to add a correction
in the drift that

01:27:08.100 --> 01:27:10.260
involves the score.

01:27:10.260 --> 01:27:12.540
And here is the intuition.

01:27:12.540 --> 01:27:16.580
The intuition is that
in the forward process,

01:27:16.580 --> 01:27:24.540
we have added some stochasticity
that made us deviate from where

01:27:24.540 --> 01:27:27.890
the actual data point was.

01:27:27.890 --> 01:27:30.370
So as a result of
that, when we want

01:27:30.370 --> 01:27:33.970
to reverse that process,
what we actually

01:27:33.970 --> 01:27:39.690
want to counteract
this phenomenon

01:27:39.690 --> 01:27:45.730
is to go towards the regions
of high density scaled

01:27:45.730 --> 01:27:47.230
by that diffusion factor.

01:27:50.210 --> 01:27:52.610
So what I'm telling you is
that the reverse process

01:27:52.610 --> 01:27:56.650
SDE is obtained through
some derivation.

01:27:56.650 --> 01:27:59.090
But the way you
would interpret it

01:27:59.090 --> 01:28:04.650
is that the correction to
the drift is due to the fact

01:28:04.650 --> 01:28:07.790
that you diffused in
the forward process.

01:28:07.790 --> 01:28:14.610
And this made you be further
from high-density regions.

01:28:14.610 --> 01:28:16.510
So this is what the
reverse process is like.

01:28:16.510 --> 01:28:19.010
So it's dx times
the drift, which

01:28:19.010 --> 01:28:22.960
is the original drift
minus the diffusion

01:28:22.960 --> 01:28:29.720
coefficient times the score
times dt plus your diffusion

01:28:29.720 --> 01:28:30.380
term.

01:28:30.380 --> 01:28:35.880
And here we noted dW bar
because the Wiener process

01:28:35.880 --> 01:28:40.160
from the reverse process
is actually different

01:28:40.160 --> 01:28:43.600
compared to the one from the
forward process, which is

01:28:43.600 --> 01:28:47.880
why we noted this with the bar.

01:28:47.880 --> 01:28:51.260
And we're just going to conclude
with how you would do inference.

01:28:51.260 --> 01:28:55.240
So to do inference, you
just sample some noise.

01:28:55.240 --> 01:29:00.320
And then again what you do is
you leverage the nice results

01:29:00.320 --> 01:29:03.120
from the fact that
you are handling SDEs,

01:29:03.120 --> 01:29:04.540
Stochastic
Differential Equations.

01:29:04.540 --> 01:29:08.960
So you, for instance, will use
a solver called Euler-Maruyama

01:29:08.960 --> 01:29:13.800
that is able to numerically
solve the reverse process.

01:29:13.800 --> 01:29:18.560
So here you can think of it
as a numerical method that

01:29:18.560 --> 01:29:23.470
will let you go from your
original ex back to the endpoint

01:29:23.470 --> 01:29:28.250
using the reverse
process equation.

01:29:28.250 --> 01:29:33.310
And you will obtain your final
image, which will hopefully

01:29:33.310 --> 01:29:36.790
be an image that is satisfying.

01:29:36.790 --> 01:29:40.570
And that's going to
be your final image.

01:29:40.570 --> 01:29:41.070
Yeah.

01:29:41.070 --> 01:29:43.530
[AUDIO OUT]

01:29:43.530 --> 01:29:45.190
Yes, so this one-- yeah.

01:29:45.190 --> 01:29:47.470
[AUDIO OUT]

01:29:47.470 --> 01:29:48.070
Yeah.

01:29:48.070 --> 01:29:51.630
So here, you can
think of this equation

01:29:51.630 --> 01:29:57.670
as being a discretization of
the reverse SDE which is here.

01:29:57.670 --> 01:30:01.730
So this is exactly this SDE, but
you're doing it step by step.

01:30:01.730 --> 01:30:04.850
[AUDIO OUT]

01:30:05.350 --> 01:30:07.150
So the question is,
what is fx of t?

01:30:07.150 --> 01:30:07.650
What is g?

01:30:07.650 --> 01:30:10.070
So these are the
coefficients that are here.

01:30:10.070 --> 01:30:15.110
So for instance, in this case, f
of x and t is minus 1/2 of beta

01:30:15.110 --> 01:30:16.470
times x.

01:30:16.470 --> 01:30:19.250
And g is square root of b.

01:30:19.250 --> 01:30:21.170
[AUDIO OUT]

01:30:21.170 --> 01:30:22.130
It is the same.

01:30:22.130 --> 01:30:24.550
So the question is,
this is for the forward.

01:30:24.550 --> 01:30:28.430
But this f and g are the same
in the forward and backwards.

01:30:28.430 --> 01:30:30.270
Yep.

01:30:30.270 --> 01:30:34.010
And with that, I'm going
to give it to Shervine.

01:30:34.010 --> 01:30:35.810
Thank you, Afshine.

01:30:35.810 --> 01:30:40.330
So I'm going to come here
and sing a very similar song

01:30:40.330 --> 01:30:43.170
as the one I sang last
week when we transitioned

01:30:43.170 --> 01:30:45.850
from DDPM to DDIM.

01:30:45.850 --> 01:30:50.010
So we're going to look at
this reverse SDE equation

01:30:50.010 --> 01:30:52.730
and look at what is
not optimal in practice

01:30:52.730 --> 01:30:55.090
and see what we can do about it.

01:30:55.090 --> 01:30:59.570
So as Afshine mentioned, you
have this Brownian motion.

01:30:59.570 --> 01:31:04.130
That is one term of that
reverse is the equation.

01:31:04.130 --> 01:31:07.010
And what problem do
we have with that?

01:31:07.010 --> 01:31:12.970
So as you saw in the transition
from x and x plus dx,

01:31:12.970 --> 01:31:18.520
you have all these spikes that
happen due to the stochasticity.

01:31:18.520 --> 01:31:22.720
And this process is the reason
why we cannot take large steps

01:31:22.720 --> 01:31:25.200
when resolving it, because
otherwise you would be

01:31:25.200 --> 01:31:29.920
approximating a movement that
is something that you look

01:31:29.920 --> 01:31:32.400
at the microscopic level.

01:31:32.400 --> 01:31:35.240
So you need a high
number of steps

01:31:35.240 --> 01:31:37.760
in your solver to start with.

01:31:37.760 --> 01:31:40.520
And also the second
point is that when

01:31:40.520 --> 01:31:43.440
you look at your
errors that you do

01:31:43.440 --> 01:31:46.300
when you solve these
reverse SDE equation,

01:31:46.300 --> 01:31:48.360
you have now two sources.

01:31:48.360 --> 01:31:50.360
You have the first
source that comes

01:31:50.360 --> 01:31:53.420
from the regular
discretization step.

01:31:53.420 --> 01:31:57.120
And then you have the
stochastic error that comes in,

01:31:57.120 --> 01:32:00.240
and they accumulate.

01:32:00.240 --> 01:32:04.320
So just like last
week, I'm going

01:32:04.320 --> 01:32:08.320
to paint the picture of what
we would like to have and see

01:32:08.320 --> 01:32:10.240
if we can get there.

01:32:10.240 --> 01:32:14.240
So what would be the
right counterpart

01:32:14.240 --> 01:32:17.230
of an SDE in a nicer world?

01:32:17.230 --> 01:32:18.510
It would be an ODE.

01:32:18.510 --> 01:32:22.610
So the ODE does have
this term on the left

01:32:22.610 --> 01:32:24.950
but doesn't have
any stochasticity.

01:32:24.950 --> 01:32:27.310
And going through each
point that I mentioned,

01:32:27.310 --> 01:32:31.750
you would be able to
gauge how fast you

01:32:31.750 --> 01:32:36.590
go depending on the regularity
on the function of the left.

01:32:36.590 --> 01:32:41.150
So you have some methods
that can take adaptive steps.

01:32:41.150 --> 01:32:43.710
And then the second
point is that in terms

01:32:43.710 --> 01:32:47.430
of errors that you're
committing, once you solve

01:32:47.430 --> 01:32:51.670
that equation, they are only
focused on the discretization

01:32:51.670 --> 01:32:52.970
of the left part.

01:32:52.970 --> 01:32:56.310
So you have less
sources of error.

01:32:56.310 --> 01:33:02.470
And I'm going to sketch you
a derivation for which only

01:33:02.470 --> 01:33:05.590
the high-level gist
is what I want you to.

01:33:05.590 --> 01:33:09.030
So there are going to be
some intermediary results

01:33:09.030 --> 01:33:11.390
that I'm just going to state.

01:33:11.390 --> 01:33:15.140
And some equations we don't
need to know necessarily

01:33:15.140 --> 01:33:16.320
how they are derived.

01:33:16.320 --> 01:33:19.540
I'm just telling you
that they are there.

01:33:19.540 --> 01:33:23.540
So let's start from
the forward SDE.

01:33:23.540 --> 01:33:27.260
As Afshine had
mentioned it, you have

01:33:27.260 --> 01:33:31.380
this f of x and t term, the
drift term, and the diffusion

01:33:31.380 --> 01:33:32.740
term.

01:33:32.740 --> 01:33:35.540
So it turns out
that for equations

01:33:35.540 --> 01:33:41.300
that verify this equality, you
can apply the Fokker-Planck

01:33:41.300 --> 01:33:46.320
equation, where you have
this pt of x that comes up.

01:33:46.320 --> 01:33:49.820
That is the probability
of the flow.

01:33:49.820 --> 01:33:52.700
And you have two terms
on the right that

01:33:52.700 --> 01:33:56.060
could be interpreted
in physical terms

01:33:56.060 --> 01:33:59.340
by the term on the left
that is a transport

01:33:59.340 --> 01:34:04.340
term and the one on the right
that you see g squared on, which

01:34:04.340 --> 01:34:07.020
is the diffusion term.

01:34:07.020 --> 01:34:09.820
So the derivation
of such an equation

01:34:09.820 --> 01:34:12.020
is non-trivial and
is out of scope here.

01:34:12.020 --> 01:34:18.360
I'm just going to state that we
are going through that results.

01:34:18.360 --> 01:34:22.280
And then by
rearranging the terms

01:34:22.280 --> 01:34:31.200
and using an identity
on pt, we can

01:34:31.200 --> 01:34:34.200
come to another equation
that is called the continuity

01:34:34.200 --> 01:34:38.040
equation that I'm
just going to tell you

01:34:38.040 --> 01:34:40.200
that it's going to
be something that we

01:34:40.200 --> 01:34:45.480
see in more details at the
beginning of next lecture.

01:34:45.480 --> 01:34:49.760
But what's interesting about
that continuity equation is

01:34:49.760 --> 01:34:54.640
that on the right
side, there is a factor

01:34:54.640 --> 01:34:57.680
that is to be interpreted
as a velocity.

01:34:57.680 --> 01:35:02.000
And the velocity is dx over dt.

01:35:02.000 --> 01:35:04.800
So once you identify
that factor,

01:35:04.800 --> 01:35:09.400
you can come up with an
equation that this term that

01:35:09.400 --> 01:35:13.950
has been identified as the
velocity needs to verify.

01:35:13.950 --> 01:35:18.950
And you come up with an
equation that is indeed an ODE,

01:35:18.950 --> 01:35:21.910
and we call it the PF-ODE.

01:35:21.910 --> 01:35:24.370
PF stands for Probability Flows.

01:35:28.110 --> 01:35:31.790
And let's look at that
equation for a bit.

01:35:31.790 --> 01:35:35.550
So just like we mentioned
a short time ago,

01:35:35.550 --> 01:35:38.910
f and g are modeling
assumptions that

01:35:38.910 --> 01:35:43.290
are the ones that we
had as assumptions

01:35:43.290 --> 01:35:48.350
once you derive this
diffusion concept.

01:35:48.350 --> 01:35:51.070
And what about the score?

01:35:51.070 --> 01:35:53.070
So the score is also
something that we can know.

01:35:53.070 --> 01:35:57.790
Because once you have a model
that can approximate this score,

01:35:57.790 --> 01:36:01.890
you just have to
put x as inputs,

01:36:01.890 --> 01:36:03.210
and you can get the score.

01:36:03.210 --> 01:36:08.980
So what I want to say is that
this whole term that popped up

01:36:08.980 --> 01:36:12.860
is all composed of
components that we can know.

01:36:16.660 --> 01:36:17.940
OK, great.

01:36:17.940 --> 01:36:21.020
And now I'm going
to re-emphasize

01:36:21.020 --> 01:36:24.060
each side of that PF-ODE.

01:36:24.060 --> 01:36:26.380
So it's an ODE we
said because we

01:36:26.380 --> 01:36:31.260
don't have a stochastic term,
and it's probability flow

01:36:31.260 --> 01:36:36.580
because it's an ODE that comes
from verifying the continuity

01:36:36.580 --> 01:36:39.580
equation, that is, an
equation that is focused

01:36:39.580 --> 01:36:42.340
on the probability flow.

01:36:42.340 --> 01:36:45.900
And one interesting
thing about that equation

01:36:45.900 --> 01:36:50.460
is that once you have an
initial condition on PT,

01:36:50.460 --> 01:36:56.100
you can fully
characterize what P is.

01:36:56.100 --> 01:37:00.900
But this is something that
you cannot say about x.

01:37:00.900 --> 01:37:06.860
Because as you can see here, if
you have v that is solution--

01:37:06.860 --> 01:37:11.850
that is part of this
equation, then if you add--

01:37:11.850 --> 01:37:18.290
if you do v plus v null, where
v null verifies divergence

01:37:18.290 --> 01:37:22.570
of v null and pt equals
0, then v plus v null also

01:37:22.570 --> 01:37:25.810
can be plugged in here.

01:37:25.810 --> 01:37:29.290
So all of that to say
that the probability flow

01:37:29.290 --> 01:37:33.850
between the reverse SDE
and here are preserved.

01:37:33.850 --> 01:37:38.110
But the trajectories are not.

01:37:38.110 --> 01:37:38.610
OK.

01:37:38.610 --> 01:37:40.670
So I went through a lot so far.

01:37:40.670 --> 01:37:42.270
Any questions?

01:37:42.270 --> 01:37:42.770
Yeah.

01:37:42.770 --> 01:37:45.710
[AUDIO OUT]

01:37:59.890 --> 01:38:00.470
Yeah, fair.

01:38:00.470 --> 01:38:02.890
So the question is, what's
the interpretation here?

01:38:02.890 --> 01:38:07.760
So we did all these equations to
conserve the probability flow,

01:38:07.760 --> 01:38:10.340
and the trajectories are
going to be different.

01:38:10.340 --> 01:38:14.480
And they are characterized
with this modified

01:38:14.480 --> 01:38:16.900
term on the v side.

01:38:16.900 --> 01:38:21.200
So instead of having these--

01:38:21.200 --> 01:38:24.160
so you have this one
half that came in,

01:38:24.160 --> 01:38:27.600
and that compensates for
the stochasticity part

01:38:27.600 --> 01:38:28.920
that disappears.

01:38:28.920 --> 01:38:31.360
So you're going to
still have trajectories

01:38:31.360 --> 01:38:35.840
that verify in aggregate the
probability flow conditions,

01:38:35.840 --> 01:38:37.760
but they are going
to be different.

01:38:37.760 --> 01:38:44.820
And the interpretation is not
average or anything specific.

01:38:44.820 --> 01:38:47.400
I would say it's just
that in aggregate, they

01:38:47.400 --> 01:38:53.220
verify the probability flow
property at each time t and x.

01:38:53.220 --> 01:38:56.020
[AUDIO OUT]

01:38:56.020 --> 01:38:56.520
Yeah.

01:38:56.520 --> 01:38:59.966
[AUDIO OUT]

01:39:00.948 --> 01:39:02.920
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:39:02.920 --> 01:39:06.620
So the question is, will they
end up with the same properties

01:39:06.620 --> 01:39:09.920
that you have the
distribution at the end?

01:39:09.920 --> 01:39:13.400
And the answer is yes,
because PT is preserved.

01:39:13.400 --> 01:39:13.900
OK.

01:39:13.900 --> 01:39:14.520
Awesome.

01:39:17.140 --> 01:39:21.340
And so I'm going to
draw a comparison

01:39:21.340 --> 01:39:26.780
between solving that reverse
SDE equation and using PF-ODE.

01:39:26.780 --> 01:39:32.220
So exactly as we mentioned
here, the term on the left,

01:39:32.220 --> 01:39:35.700
like the drift term, is
compensated for that lack

01:39:35.700 --> 01:39:38.840
of stochastic term.

01:39:38.840 --> 01:39:41.660
And then the great
point that you made

01:39:41.660 --> 01:39:44.400
is that we no longer have
something that is stochastic.

01:39:44.400 --> 01:39:50.100
But once you have an
initial condition,

01:39:50.100 --> 01:39:53.300
you deterministically
know where you're going.

01:39:53.300 --> 01:39:56.100
So that's one big
difference that you

01:39:56.100 --> 01:39:58.793
might remember from last
time resembles something

01:39:58.793 --> 01:40:00.500
that you know.

01:40:00.500 --> 01:40:05.010
And as a result, since the
only source of diversity

01:40:05.010 --> 01:40:11.570
is now your initial draw, you
have less sampling diversity.

01:40:11.570 --> 01:40:15.970
And interestingly, when
you look at experiments,

01:40:15.970 --> 01:40:18.730
you have higher sample quality.

01:40:18.730 --> 01:40:23.650
And that could be explained
intuitively by the fact

01:40:23.650 --> 01:40:27.090
that you introduce
noise at each time step

01:40:27.090 --> 01:40:30.330
enables you to explore
regions of the space

01:40:30.330 --> 01:40:33.730
that you wouldn't have explored
with a deterministic path.

01:40:33.730 --> 01:40:38.890
And you may self-correct
your trajectory, as opposed

01:40:38.890 --> 01:40:43.610
to resolving an ODE
that is only conditioned

01:40:43.610 --> 01:40:46.850
on your initial points
and all the errors

01:40:46.850 --> 01:40:51.290
that you accumulate by
solving the ODE numerically.

01:40:51.290 --> 01:40:54.890
So you have a nice
interpretation there.

01:40:54.890 --> 01:40:58.730
And of course, now that
we have an ODE where

01:40:58.730 --> 01:41:03.200
we can adapt step
sizes, you can now

01:41:03.200 --> 01:41:06.880
resolve this faster,
which is a nice property.

01:41:06.880 --> 01:41:08.840
And the fact that
I told you that I'm

01:41:08.840 --> 01:41:11.960
drawing a very similar
parallel between PF-ODE

01:41:11.960 --> 01:41:16.480
and the reverse SDE as the one
I drew last week between DDIM

01:41:16.480 --> 01:41:20.160
and DDPM, we can see a lot
of shared commonalities

01:41:20.160 --> 01:41:24.840
that I've put here
for reference.

01:41:24.840 --> 01:41:29.260
And now let's look at how we can
solve this equation in practice

01:41:29.260 --> 01:41:33.280
and if we can do even
better than regular solvers.

01:41:33.280 --> 01:41:37.080
In order to do that, I'm
going to introduce a way

01:41:37.080 --> 01:41:39.920
to assess complexity
that is widely used

01:41:39.920 --> 01:41:42.560
in these papers called NFE.

01:41:42.560 --> 01:41:45.000
So it's the number of
function evaluations.

01:41:45.000 --> 01:41:48.080
And what we want to
do is for a sampler

01:41:48.080 --> 01:41:53.640
to get good quality results
with a minimum amount of NFEs.

01:41:53.640 --> 01:41:56.960
So it's a measure that makes
intuitive sense in the sense

01:41:56.960 --> 01:42:00.350
that we want to minimize
the number of forward passes

01:42:00.350 --> 01:42:04.030
that you perform on your model.

01:42:04.030 --> 01:42:09.790
And I'm going to introduce
this notion of solving

01:42:09.790 --> 01:42:12.950
an ODE by the very simple
method that some of you

01:42:12.950 --> 01:42:16.230
might know already,
so Euler's method.

01:42:16.230 --> 01:42:22.630
If you consider this ODE
dx equals v of x and t dt,

01:42:22.630 --> 01:42:25.350
you can, based on
your initial point

01:42:25.350 --> 01:42:27.430
and the derivative
at that point,

01:42:27.430 --> 01:42:33.270
approximate the next point.

01:42:33.270 --> 01:42:37.490
But the issue with that is that
there is quite a bit of errors.

01:42:37.490 --> 01:42:39.650
Even though it's just
one NFE per step,

01:42:39.650 --> 01:42:43.950
you end up with the results
that might be imprecise.

01:42:43.950 --> 01:42:48.830
So people to account for
that fact are using a fancier

01:42:48.830 --> 01:42:54.510
techniques, so Runge-Kutta
4 is one of them that--

01:42:54.510 --> 01:42:58.390
so I represent it here on this
graph the intuition of it.

01:42:58.390 --> 01:43:03.890
So you look at midpoints,
measure the derivative,

01:43:03.890 --> 01:43:06.790
have some derivative measurement
on the endpoint as well.

01:43:06.790 --> 01:43:10.210
And if you do some weighting
out of all of this,

01:43:10.210 --> 01:43:16.350
you obtain an update step
that is of low error rate.

01:43:16.350 --> 01:43:22.490
I think here it's O of delta
t to the power of 5, which

01:43:22.490 --> 01:43:23.110
is nice.

01:43:23.110 --> 01:43:26.610
So you might tell me,
OK, hey, Shervine, we

01:43:26.610 --> 01:43:27.670
have solved everything.

01:43:27.670 --> 01:43:30.370
We have a nice sampling method.

01:43:30.370 --> 01:43:33.530
What could we do better?

01:43:33.530 --> 01:43:38.330
So actually let's look
again at v of x and t.

01:43:38.330 --> 01:43:44.150
So I told you it's f of x and
t minus this term on the right

01:43:44.150 --> 01:43:46.250
that is divided by 2.

01:43:46.250 --> 01:43:48.970
But if you look at
all the equations

01:43:48.970 --> 01:43:51.530
that we use in the
diffusion world,

01:43:51.530 --> 01:44:02.960
you see that f and x and t can
be rewritten as f of t times x.

01:44:02.960 --> 01:44:04.800
So what does that tell you?

01:44:04.800 --> 01:44:10.920
So that tells you that
this ODE is linear in x.

01:44:10.920 --> 01:44:12.460
So it has a linear term.

01:44:15.400 --> 01:44:18.200
And what can you say about
the term on the right?

01:44:18.200 --> 01:44:21.880
Is it linear or--

01:44:21.880 --> 01:44:26.440
so you would have x
as an input to where?

01:44:26.440 --> 01:44:27.980
What is the term on the right?

01:44:27.980 --> 01:44:31.320
The one in the rectangle.

01:44:31.320 --> 01:44:33.120
It's the score.

01:44:33.120 --> 01:44:35.240
So the score is what
we try to estimate

01:44:35.240 --> 01:44:39.320
as part of our reverse
diffusion process.

01:44:39.320 --> 01:44:41.040
And it's typically
something that you

01:44:41.040 --> 01:44:43.280
would approximate
with a neural network.

01:44:43.280 --> 01:44:46.800
And neural networks,
are they linear?

01:44:46.800 --> 01:44:48.940
No, they're highly nonlinear.

01:44:48.940 --> 01:44:51.440
After the first layer and
first activation function,

01:44:51.440 --> 01:44:53.870
it's no longer linear.

01:44:53.870 --> 01:44:57.310
So you have a
nonlinear term there.

01:44:57.310 --> 01:45:01.470
And one thing we
could do is instead

01:45:01.470 --> 01:45:04.550
of trying to discretize
this whole equation

01:45:04.550 --> 01:45:09.550
without leveraging the fact
that part of it is linear,

01:45:09.550 --> 01:45:13.630
we can use some
math properties that

01:45:13.630 --> 01:45:17.750
says that with methods like
the variations of constants

01:45:17.750 --> 01:45:19.310
that some of you
might have heard of,

01:45:19.310 --> 01:45:23.430
you can derive an exact
solution to part of it

01:45:23.430 --> 01:45:28.790
and focus the discretization
process on the other part.

01:45:28.790 --> 01:45:32.670
And that's what DPM
solver is all about.

01:45:32.670 --> 01:45:35.930
And I'm going to spare you
the details of the derivation.

01:45:35.930 --> 01:45:36.990
It's quite long.

01:45:36.990 --> 01:45:39.270
I have attached the paper
at the bottom in case

01:45:39.270 --> 01:45:40.630
you're interested.

01:45:40.630 --> 01:45:41.910
It's a bit involved.

01:45:41.910 --> 01:45:48.350
You get an update formula that
has this exact term on the left

01:45:48.350 --> 01:45:50.910
and then some
integral on the right.

01:45:50.910 --> 01:45:57.460
And the integral on the right
focuses On the nonlinear term

01:45:57.460 --> 01:45:59.980
that we saw.

01:45:59.980 --> 01:46:02.820
And one highlight
that I'll mention here

01:46:02.820 --> 01:46:06.240
is that we are not solving
this in the time space.

01:46:06.240 --> 01:46:07.880
There has been a
change of variables,

01:46:07.880 --> 01:46:10.940
and this is what the lambda
that you see in this equation

01:46:10.940 --> 01:46:12.220
stands for.

01:46:12.220 --> 01:46:13.940
And there is a
one-to-one mapping

01:46:13.940 --> 01:46:16.980
between time steps and lambdas.

01:46:16.980 --> 01:46:21.980
Because lambda is
strictly monotonic here.

01:46:21.980 --> 01:46:27.020
So yeah, this is one
interesting thing to note.

01:46:27.020 --> 01:46:33.340
And the question now is, how
do we discretize this equation?

01:46:33.340 --> 01:46:36.220
Because we have this
integral now left.

01:46:36.220 --> 01:46:41.060
And we cannot integrate
something that is approximated

01:46:41.060 --> 01:46:45.980
by a neural network
over a continuous range.

01:46:45.980 --> 01:46:48.460
So what we do is that
this is the part that

01:46:48.460 --> 01:46:50.360
is going to be discretized.

01:46:50.360 --> 01:46:54.000
And you have variants
of DPM solver

01:46:54.000 --> 01:46:57.460
that discretize it at different
levels of granularity.

01:46:57.460 --> 01:47:00.560
So the first one is DPM
solver 1 that the paper

01:47:00.560 --> 01:47:06.120
shows gives an equation that
is equivalent to DDIM actually.

01:47:06.120 --> 01:47:11.240
And DPM solver 2 uses
Taylor's expansion

01:47:11.240 --> 01:47:15.520
at higher levels
up to any number k.

01:47:15.520 --> 01:47:20.960
And the number-- so the NFE
for each is of I plus 1--

01:47:23.760 --> 01:47:24.900
is of I, sorry.

01:47:28.000 --> 01:47:32.640
And so to recap what we
did is that we started

01:47:32.640 --> 01:47:37.680
from the forward SDE, rearranged
terms using properties

01:47:37.680 --> 01:47:38.960
that we knew of.

01:47:38.960 --> 01:47:42.120
Came up with a PF-ODE that
verified the same probability

01:47:42.120 --> 01:47:42.840
flow.

01:47:42.840 --> 01:47:46.800
And that enabled us to
generate new samples.

01:47:46.800 --> 01:47:50.790
And then we leveraged
the format of the PF-ODE

01:47:50.790 --> 01:47:56.670
to further optimize the
way we would resolve it

01:47:56.670 --> 01:48:01.870
by focusing our NFEs on the
discretization parts that really

01:48:01.870 --> 01:48:03.510
matters here.

01:48:03.510 --> 01:48:09.910
And here you see that DPM
solver resolves the ODE

01:48:09.910 --> 01:48:14.630
with a much better trade-off
between budget and quality.

01:48:14.630 --> 01:48:16.310
And the difference
on the paper you

01:48:16.310 --> 01:48:19.350
can see it at low
number of steps

01:48:19.350 --> 01:48:22.550
they're quite huge
in terms of N metric.

01:48:22.550 --> 01:48:24.390
And then just like
DDIM, you don't

01:48:24.390 --> 01:48:27.190
need to retrain the
network when you

01:48:27.190 --> 01:48:32.030
want to sample with this solver
because the objective function

01:48:32.030 --> 01:48:33.190
is the same.

01:48:33.190 --> 01:48:36.370
And in practice, with
only tens of NFEs,

01:48:36.370 --> 01:48:39.550
you can get good results.

01:48:39.550 --> 01:48:40.490
And that was it.

01:48:40.490 --> 01:48:42.660
I hope you have a great weekend.

